// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2004, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"With reference to Indian Public Finance, consider the following statements:\nDisbursements from Public Accounts of India are subject to the Vote of Parliament.\nThe Indian Constitution provides for the establishment of a Consolidated Fund, a Public Account and a Contingency Fund for each State.\nAppropriations and disbursements under the Railway Budget are subject to the same form of parliamentary control as other appropriations and disbursements.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Consolidated Fund","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Public Account withdrawals are not subject to parliamentary vote in the same manner as withdrawals from the Consolidated Fund.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The Constitution provides these funds/accounts for the Union and States, but the statement's formulation is not accepted by the source key.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Railway expenditure is subject to parliamentary control through the budgetary process.","revisionPoints":"Consolidated Fund: Main government account; withdrawals generally require parliamentary appropriation.\nPublic Account: Government acts mainly as a banker/trustee.\nContingency Fund: Used for unforeseen expenditure.\nRailway Budget was historically presented separately but remained subject to parliamentary control."},
{"no":2,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Vijayanagara Empire:\nNarasimha Saluva ended the Sangama dynasty and started the Saluva dynasty.\nVira Narasimha deposed the last Saluva ruler and seized the throne.\nVira Narasimha was succeeded by his younger brother, Krishnadeva Raya.\nKrishnadeva Raya was succeeded by his half-brother, Achyuta Raya.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Vijayanagara Empire","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Narasimha Saluva established the Saluva dynasty after ending Sangama rule.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Vira Narasimha displaced the Saluva ruler and established the Tuluva dynasty.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Krishnadeva Raya succeeded his brother Vira Narasimha.\nStatement 4 — Correct: Achyuta Raya succeeded Krishnadeva Raya.","revisionPoints":"Vijayanagara had four major dynasties:\nSangama\nSaluva\nTuluva\nAravidu\nKrishnadeva Raya: Most prominent ruler of the Tuluva dynasty.\nAchyuta Raya: Succeeded Krishnadeva Raya."},
{"no":3,"text":"Some of the main features of the Government of India Act, 1935 were:\nAbolition of diarchy in the Governors' provinces.\nPower of Governors to veto legislative action and to legislate on their own.\nAbolition of communal representation.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Government of India Act 1935","tags":["GoI Act 1935","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Provincial diarchy introduced by the Government of India Act, 1919 was abolished.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Governors retained significant discretionary and special powers.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: Communal representation was not abolished; separate electorates continued and were extended to some groups.","revisionPoints":"Government of India Act, 1935:\nProvincial autonomy\nAbolition of provincial diarchy\nProposed federation\nExtended communal representation\nStrong discretionary powers for Governors\nIt became an important constitutional precursor to independent India."},
{"no":4,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe First Session of the Indian National Congress was held in Calcutta.\nThe Second Session of the Indian National Congress was held under the presidentship of Dadabhai Naoroji.\nBoth the Indian National Congress and Muslim League held their sessions at Lucknow in 1916 and concluded the Lucknow Pact.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Indian National Congress","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: The first INC session was held at Bombay in 1885, not Calcutta.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Dadabhai Naoroji presided over the second INC session at Calcutta in 1886.\nStatement 3 — Correct: INC and Muslim League sessions were held at Lucknow in 1916, leading to the Lucknow Pact.","revisionPoints":"1885: First INC session — Bombay.\nFirst President — W.C. Bonnerjee.\n1886: Second session — Calcutta; President — Dadabhai Naoroji.\n1916: Lucknow Pact between Congress and Muslim League."},
{"no":5,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The Constituent Assembly of India was elected by the Provincial Assemblies in 1946.","B":"Jawaharlal Nehru, M.A. Jinnah and Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel were members of the Constituent Assembly of India.","C":"The First Session of the Constituent Assembly was held in January 1947.","D":"The Constitution of India was adopted on 26 January 1950.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Constituent Assembly","tags":["Constituent Assembly","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Option (a) — Correct: Members representing British Indian provinces were indirectly elected by provincial legislatures in 1946.\nOption (b) — Incorrect: Jinnah was not a member of the Indian Constituent Assembly.\nOption (c) — Incorrect: The first meeting was held on 9 December 1946.\nOption (d) — Incorrect: The Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950.","revisionPoints":"Constituent Assembly formed under the Cabinet Mission Plan, 1946.\nFirst meeting: 9 December 1946.\nTemporary Chairman: Sachchidananda Sinha.\nPermanent President: Dr. Rajendra Prasad.\nConstitution adopted: 26 November 1949.\nConstitution commenced: 26 January 1950."},
{"no":6,"text":"The Archaeological Survey of India is an attached office of the Department/Ministry of:","A":"Culture","B":"Tourism","C":"Science and Technology","D":"Human Resource Development","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Archaeological Survey of India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) is associated with the Ministry dealing with culture and heritage.\nIt is responsible for archaeological research, conservation and protection of monuments.","revisionPoints":"ASI was established in 1861.\nMajor functions:\nArchaeological research\nMonument conservation\nProtection of archaeological sites\nExcavation and documentation\nImportant centrally protected monuments come under ASI."},
{"no":7,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe highest criminal court of the district is the Court of District and Sessions Judge.\nDistrict Judges are appointed by the Governor in consultation with the High Court.\nA person is eligible for appointment as a District Judge if they have been an advocate or pleader for at least seven years, or are an officer in judicial service of the Union or State.\nWhen a Sessions Judge awards a death sentence, it must be confirmed by the High Court before execution.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["District Judge","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: District and Sessions Court is the highest district-level court for civil and criminal jurisdiction.\nStatement 2 — Correct: District judges are appointed by the Governor in consultation with the High Court.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The Constitution provides the relevant eligibility requirement of seven years' practice.\nStatement 4 — Correct: A death sentence awarded by a Sessions Court requires confirmation by the High Court.","revisionPoints":"Article 233: Appointment of district judges.\nGovernor appoints in consultation with the High Court.\nSessions Court handles serious criminal cases.\nDeath sentence requires High Court confirmation."},
{"no":8,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Speaker of Lok Sabha can adjourn the House sine die, but after prorogation only the President can summon the House.\nUnless sooner dissolved or extended, Lok Sabha automatically dissolves after five years by efflux of time.\nThe Speaker continues in office after dissolution until immediately before the first meeting of the new House.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Speaker of Lok Sabha","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Adjournment is a House procedure, while prorogation is done by the President.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Lok Sabha normally has a five-year term unless dissolved earlier or extended constitutionally during an emergency.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect according to the source key: The Speaker's continuation after dissolution is subject to constitutional provisions and the wording in the statement is not accepted here.","revisionPoints":"Adjournment: Presiding officer.\nProrogation: President.\nDissolution: Applies to Lok Sabha, not Rajya Sabha.\nNormal Lok Sabha term: 5 years.\nRajya Sabha is a permanent House."},
{"no":9,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Under TPDS, BPL families are provided 50 kg of food grains per month per family at subsidised price.","B":"Under Annapurna Scheme, eligible indigent senior citizens can receive 10 kg of food grains per person per month free of cost.","C":"Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment provides 15 kg of food grains per person per month to indigent people in welfare institutions.","D":"Ministry of Human Resource Development provides financial support to the Midday Meal Scheme for Classes I–V in government or government-aided schools.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Targeted Public Distribution System","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies Option (a) as correct.\nTPDS was designed to provide subsidised food grains to identified poor households.\nThe other schemes had different eligibility conditions and institutional arrangements.","revisionPoints":"TPDS: Targeted poor households.\nBPL: Below Poverty Line.\nAnnapurna Scheme: Food security support for eligible indigent elderly persons.\nMid-Day Meal: Nutritional support for schoolchildren."},
{"no":10,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nNon-function of the lachrymal gland is an important symptom of Vitamin A deficiency.\nVitamin B1 deficiency can lead to indigestion and heart enlargement.\nVitamin C deficiency can lead to pain in the muscles.\nVitamin D deficiency causes increased loss of Ca²⁺ in urine.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Vitamin Deficiency","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Vitamin A deficiency can affect lacrimal gland function and cause dryness of the eye.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Vitamin B1 deficiency is associated with beriberi and cardiovascular/neurological manifestations.\nStatement 3 — Not accepted by the source key.\nStatement 4 — Not accepted by the source key.","revisionPoints":"Vitamin A: Vision and epithelial health.\nVitamin B1 (Thiamine): Carbohydrate metabolism and nervous/cardiac function.\nVitamin C: Collagen synthesis; deficiency causes scurvy.\nVitamin D: Calcium-phosphorus metabolism and bone health."},
{"no":11,"text":"The hormone insulin is a:","A":"Glycolipid","B":"Fatty acid","C":"Peptide","D":"Steroid","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Insulin","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Insulin is a peptide/protein hormone.\nIt is secreted by the β-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas.\nIt helps reduce blood glucose by promoting glucose uptake and storage.","revisionPoints":"Insulin → Pancreatic β-cells.\nMain role → lowers blood glucose.\nGlucagon → pancreatic α-cells → raises blood glucose.\nInsulin deficiency/dysfunction is associated with diabetes mellitus."},
{"no":12,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nToothless mammals such as pangolins are not found in India.\nGibbon is the only ape found in India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Wildlife of India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Pangolins are found in India.\nStatement 2 — Correct according to the source: The gibbon is the ape species represented in India's fauna.","revisionPoints":"Indian pangolins are mammals with protective scales.\nHoolock gibbon is India's only native ape.\nGibbons are found mainly in northeastern India.\nApes differ from monkeys in several anatomical and evolutionary characteristics."},
{"no":13,"text":"In which organ of the human body are lymphocyte cells formed?","A":"Liver","B":"Long bone","C":"Pancreas","D":"Spleen","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Lymphocytes","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Lymphocytes originate from hematopoietic stem cells in bone marrow.\nB-lymphocytes mature in the bone marrow.\nT-lymphocytes originate in bone marrow and mature in the thymus.","revisionPoints":"Bone marrow: Major site of blood-cell formation.\nB cells: Mature in bone marrow.\nT cells: Mature in thymus.\nSpleen and lymph nodes: Important secondary lymphoid organs."},
{"no":14,"text":"In which one of the following Union Territories do the people of the Onge tribe live?","A":"Andaman and Nicobar Islands","B":"Dadra and Nagar Haveli","C":"Daman and Diu","D":"Lakshadweep","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Tribes & Union Territories","tags":["Onge Tribe","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The Onge are an Indigenous tribal community of the Andaman Islands.\nThey are among India's particularly vulnerable tribal communities.","revisionPoints":"Onge → Andaman Islands.\nOther important Andaman tribal communities include:\nGreat Andamanese\nJarawa\nSentinelese\nThe Andaman and Nicobar Islands have distinctive tribal and ecological diversity."},
{"no":15,"text":"Consider the following crops:\nCotton\nGroundnut\nMaize\nMustard\nWhich of the above are Kharif crops?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Kharif Crops","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Cotton — Kharif: Generally sown with the monsoon.\nGroundnut — Kharif: Major kharif oilseed crop.\nMaize — Kharif: Widely cultivated during the monsoon season.\nMustard — Rabi: Generally sown during the winter season.","revisionPoints":"Kharif: Monsoon-season crops.\nExamples: rice, maize, cotton, groundnut, soybean.\nRabi: Winter-season crops.\nExamples: wheat, mustard, gram, barley.\nZaid: Short summer cropping season."},
{"no":16,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"In Lok Sabha, a no-confidence motion has to set out the grounds on which it is based.","B":"No conditions of admissibility have been laid down for a no-confidence motion.","C":"Once admitted, a no-confidence motion has to be taken up within ten days of leave being granted.","D":"Rajya Sabha is not empowered to entertain a motion of no confidence.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["No Confidence Motion","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies (a) as correct.\nA no-confidence motion concerns whether the Council of Ministers continues to enjoy the confidence of Lok Sabha.\nIt is a parliamentary mechanism for testing the government's majority.","revisionPoints":"Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to Lok Sabha.\nNo-confidence motion is moved in Lok Sabha.\nRajya Sabha cannot remove the Council of Ministers through a no-confidence motion.\nIt differs from a confidence motion, which is generally initiated by the government."},
{"no":17,"text":"Which one of the following statements correctly describes the Fourth Schedule of the Constitution of India?","A":"It contains the scheme of distribution of powers between Union and States.","B":"It contains the languages listed in the Constitution.","C":"It contains provisions regarding administration of tribal areas.","D":"It allocates seats in the Council of States.","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Fourth Schedule","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The Fourth Schedule deals with allocation of seats in the Rajya Sabha among States and Union Territories.\nIt is therefore directly connected with representation in the Council of States.","revisionPoints":"4th Schedule: Allocation of Rajya Sabha seats.\n7th Schedule: Union, State and Concurrent Lists.\n8th Schedule: Recognised languages.\n5th Schedule: Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes.\n6th Schedule: Tribal areas in specified northeastern States."},
{"no":18,"text":"Who was the last ruler of the Tughluq dynasty of the Delhi Sultanate?","A":"Firuz Shah Tughluq","B":"Ghiyasuddin Tughluq Shah II","C":"Nasiruddin Mahmud","D":"Nasrat Shah","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Delhi Sultanate","Tughluq Dynasty","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Nasiruddin Mahmud Shah Tughluq was the last significant ruler of the Tughluq dynasty.\nThe dynasty weakened considerably after Firuz Shah Tughluq.\nThe invasion of Timur in 1398 further weakened the Delhi Sultanate.","revisionPoints":"Tughluq dynasty began with Ghiyasuddin Tughluq.\nMuhammad bin Tughluq: Known for administrative experiments.\nFiruz Shah Tughluq: Major public works and welfare measures.\nTughluq decline → political fragmentation and emergence of the Sayyid dynasty."},
{"no":19,"text":"The great Asian river Mekong does run through:","A":"China","B":"Malaysia","C":"Cambodia","D":"Laos","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Mekong River","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The uploaded source gives (b) as the answer.\nHowever, the question/key contains a geographical inconsistency: the Mekong flows through China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam, not Malaysia.\nTherefore, the source answer should be treated as a source-key error rather than a reliable geographical fact.","revisionPoints":"Mekong originates in the Tibetan Plateau.\nIt flows through:\nChina\nMyanmar\nLaos\nThailand\nCambodia\nVietnam\nIt is one of Southeast Asia's major transboundary rivers.\nMalaysia is not a Mekong-riparian country."},
{"no":20,"text":"Which one of the following was the largest IT software and services exporter in India during the year 2002–03?","A":"BirlaSoft","B":"Infosys Technologies","C":"Tata Consultancy Services","D":"Wipro Technologies","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"IT & Services","tags":["IT Exports","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies Tata Consultancy Services (TCS) as the largest IT software and services exporter for 2002–03.\nTCS was one of India's major early global IT services companies.","revisionPoints":"TCS: Part of the Tata Group.\nIndian IT growth was driven strongly by:\nSoftware exports\nIT-enabled services\nGlobal outsourcing\nOther major Indian IT companies include Infosys and Wipro.\nFor current rankings, historical 2002–03 data should not be confused with present-day rankings."},
{"no":21,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The largest Buddhist monastery in India is in Assam.","B":"The language Konyak is spoken in Nagaland.","C":"The largest river island in the world is in Assam.","D":"Sikkim is the least populated state of the Indian Union.","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Indian States","tags":["North East India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement (a) — Correct as per the source key.\nThe question is based on geographical and cultural facts concerning northeastern India.\nThe source identifies Assam as the location of the largest Buddhist monastery.","revisionPoints":"Northeast India has significant Buddhist cultural influence.\nAssam is known for its monasteries, locally called Satras in the Vaishnavite tradition.\nKonyak is a major Naga language associated with Nagaland.\nSikkim has historically had a very small population among Indian States."},
{"no":22,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Oil Pool Account of Government of India was dismantled with effect from 1 April 2002.\nSubsidies on PDS kerosene and domestic LPG are borne by the Consolidated Fund of India.\nThe Mashelkar Committee recommended that Bharat Stage II emission norms should be applied throughout the country by April 1, 2004.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy Policy","tags":["Oil Pool Account","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source key.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Government support for specified petroleum products was handled through the public-finance mechanism described in the question.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The Mashelkar Committee was associated with India's national auto-fuel policy and emission norms.","revisionPoints":"Bharat Stage norms: Vehicle emission standards.\nAuto-fuel policy deals with:\nVehicle emissions\nFuel quality\nPollution control\nMashelkar Committee: Associated with India's auto-fuel policy recommendations."},
{"no":23,"text":"In the last one decade, which one among the following sectors attracted the highest Foreign Direct Investment inflows into India?","A":"Chemicals other than fertilisers","B":"Services sector","C":"Food processing","D":"Telecommunication","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Foreign Direct Investment","tags":["FDI","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies telecommunication as the sector receiving the highest FDI inflows during the period referred to.\nTelecom liberalisation created significant opportunities for foreign investment.","revisionPoints":"FDI: Investment by a foreign entity in an enterprise in another country with a lasting interest.\nTelecom was a major recipient of foreign investment during India's liberalisation period.\nFDI inflows vary considerably across sectors and time periods."},
{"no":24,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Chinese pilgrim Fa-Hien attended the Fourth Great Buddhist Council held by Kanishka.\nThe Chinese pilgrim Hiuen Tsang met Harsha and found him to be antagonistic to Buddhism.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhist Councils","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Fa-Hien lived much earlier than Hiuen Tsang and was not associated with Kanishka's Buddhist Council.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: Hiuen Tsang's account portrays Harsha as a patron of Buddhism, not antagonistic towards it.\nTherefore, neither statement is correct.","revisionPoints":"Fa-Hien: Visited India during the Gupta period, particularly during Chandragupta II's reign.\nHiuen Tsang: Visited India during Harsha's reign.\nHarsha supported Buddhism while also extending patronage to other religious traditions.\nKanishka is traditionally associated with the Fourth Buddhist Council."},
{"no":25,"text":"How did the dynasty of Nizam Shahis of Ahmednagar come to an end?","A":"Ahmednagar was annexed into the Mughal Empire and Husain Shah was consigned to life imprisonment.","B":"Mughal troops destroyed Daulatabad fort and killed Nizamul Mulk of Ahmednagar.","C":"Fateh Khan usurped the throne from Nizamul Mulk.","D":"Malik Ambar was defeated in a battle with Mughals in 1631 and the entire royal family was killed by Mughal troops.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Nizam Shahi Dynasty","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The Nizam Shahi kingdom of Ahmednagar was gradually weakened by Mughal expansion.\nMughal emperor Shah Jahan completed the annexation of Ahmednagar.\nThe ruling family was removed from effective political power.","revisionPoints":"Ahmednagar was one of the Deccan Sultanates.\nImportant Deccan Sultanates:\nAhmednagar\nBijapur\nGolconda\nBidar\nBerar\nMalik Ambar was a major military and administrative figure of Ahmednagar.\nMughal expansion into the Deccan intensified under Akbar, Jahangir and Shah Jahan."},
{"no":26,"text":"With reference to ancient Jainism, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Jainism was spread in South India under the leadership of Sthalabahu.","B":"The Jains who remained under the leadership of Bhadrabahu were called Shvetambaras after the Council held at Pataliputra.","C":"Jainism enjoyed the patronage of the Kalinga king Kharavela in the first century BC.","D":"In the initial stage of Jainism, Jains worshipped images unlike Buddhists.","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Jainism","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement (b) — Source answer.\nThe question refers to the traditional account of divisions within Jainism.\nThe source associates the group led by Bhadrabahu with the development of the Jain sectarian division.","revisionPoints":"Two major Jain traditions:\nDigambara\nShvetambara\nJainism originated in the 6th century BCE context of the Śramaṇa traditions.\nMahavira is regarded as the 24th Tirthankara.\nKharavela of Kalinga was an important patron of Jainism."},
{"no":27,"text":"Which one of the following four Vedas contains an account of magical charms and spells?","A":"Rigveda","B":"Yajurveda","C":"Atharvaveda","D":"Samaveda","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Vedas","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Atharvaveda contains hymns, prayers, charms and spells.\nIt deals more extensively with everyday life, health, healing and certain magical practices than the other Vedas.","revisionPoints":"Rigveda: Hymns and praises of deities.\nSamaveda: Musical/chanted versions of mainly Rigvedic verses.\nYajurveda: Sacrificial formulas and rituals.\nAtharvaveda: Charms, healing, domestic life and magical practices."},
{"no":28,"text":"The Montague-Chelmsford Report formed the basis of:","A":"Indian Councils Act, 1909","B":"Government of India Act, 1919","C":"Government of India Act, 1935","D":"Indian Independence Act, 1947","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Montague-Chelmsford Reforms","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The Montague-Chelmsford Report proposed constitutional reforms for British India.\nIts recommendations formed the basis of the Government of India Act, 1919.\nThe Act introduced dyarchy in the provinces.","revisionPoints":"Morley-Minto Reforms → 1909 Act\nMontague-Chelmsford Reforms → 1919 Act\nSimon Commission + Round Table Conferences + White Paper → 1935 Act\nGovernment of India Act, 1919 introduced provincial dyarchy."},
{"no":29,"text":"During the Indian Freedom Struggle, who proposed that Swaraj should be defined as complete independence free from all foreign control?","A":"Mazharul Haque","B":"Maulana Hasrat Mohani","C":"Hakim Ajmal Khan","D":"Abul Kalam Azad","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Swaraj","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Hasrat Mohani was an early advocate of complete independence.\nHe is associated with the demand for Purna Swaraj before it became the official Congress objective.","revisionPoints":"Hasrat Mohani: Associated with the demand for complete independence.\n1929 Lahore Session: Congress officially adopted Purna Swaraj as its goal.\n26 January 1930: Observed as Independence Day following the Lahore resolution.\n1931 Karachi Session: Focused on Fundamental Rights and the National Economic Programme."},
{"no":30,"text":"Which one of the following sequences indicates the correct chronological order?","A":"Shankaracharya → Ramanuja → Chaitanya","B":"Ramanuja → Shankaracharya → Chaitanya","C":"Ramanuja → Chaitanya → Shankaracharya","D":"Shankaracharya → Chaitanya → Ramanuja","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient & Medieval India","tags":["Bhakti Movement","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source gives (d) as the answer.\nHowever, the chronological ordering in the extracted question appears inconsistent with the commonly accepted historical dates.\nTherefore, the source key should be treated cautiously.","revisionPoints":"Shankaracharya: Advaita Vedanta.\nRamanuja: Vishishtadvaita Vedanta.\nChaitanya: Bhakti movement; devotion centred on Krishna.\nUPSC chronology questions require attention to dates and sequence, not only ideological associations."},
{"no":31,"text":"Consider the following Princely States:\nJhansi\nSambalpur\nSatara\nWhat was the correct chronological order in which they were annexed by the British?","A":"1-2-3","B":"1-3-2","C":"3-2-1","D":"3-1-2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Doctrine of Lapse","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Satara was annexed first under the Doctrine of Lapse.\nSambalpur followed.\nJhansi was annexed later.\nThese annexations are associated with Lord Dalhousie's expansionist policy.","revisionPoints":"Doctrine of Lapse: Associated with Lord Dalhousie.\nImportant annexations:\nSatara\nSambalpur\nJhansi\nNagpur\nJhansi became a major centre of resistance during the Revolt of 1857."},
{"no":32,"text":"Who returned the Knighthood conferred on him by the British Government as a protest against the atrocities in Punjab in 1919?","A":"Tej Bahadur Sapru","B":"Ashutosh Mukherjee","C":"Rabindranath Tagore","D":"Syed Ahmad Khan","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Jallianwala Bagh","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Rabindranath Tagore renounced his knighthood in 1919.\nHe did so in protest against the British response to the Jallianwala Bagh massacre and the repression in Punjab.","revisionPoints":"Jallianwala Bagh massacre: 13 April 1919.\nOccurred at Amritsar under General Reginald Dyer.\nTagore renounced his knighthood as a protest.\nThe event became a major turning point in India's anti-colonial movement."},
{"no":33,"text":"The resolution for removing the Vice-President of India can be moved in the:","A":"Lok Sabha alone","B":"Either House of Parliament","C":"Joint Sitting of Parliament","D":"Rajya Sabha alone","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Vice President","tags":["Vice President of India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The Vice-President is the ex officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha.\nA resolution for removal must originate in the Rajya Sabha.\nIt must then be agreed to by the Lok Sabha.","revisionPoints":"Article 63: Office of Vice-President.\nArticle 64: Vice-President is ex officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha.\nRemoval resolution originates in Rajya Sabha.\nUnlike impeachment of the President, this is not called impeachment."},
{"no":34,"text":"With reference to the Constitution of India, which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?","A":"Forests — Concurrent List","B":"Stock Exchanges — Concurrent List","C":"Post Office Saving Bank — Union List","D":"Public Health — State List","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Seventh Schedule","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Forests are in the Concurrent List.\nPublic health is primarily a State List subject.\nStock exchanges are associated with the Union List, not the Concurrent List.\nHence (b) is the incorrectly matched pair.","revisionPoints":"Union List: Matters of national importance.\nState List: Public order, public health, agriculture etc.\nConcurrent List: Both Parliament and State Legislatures can legislate.\nForests were transferred from State List to Concurrent List by the 42nd Amendment, 1976."},
{"no":35,"text":"Consider the following tasks:\nSuperintendence, direction and conduct of free and fair elections.\nPreparation of electoral rolls for elections to Parliament, State Legislatures, President and Vice-President.\nRecognition of political parties and allotment of election symbols.\nProclamation of final verdict in election disputes.\nWhich of the above are functions of the Election Commission of India?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Election Commission","tags":["Election Commission of India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"1 — Correct: ECI supervises and conducts elections within its constitutional jurisdiction.\n2 — Correct: Electoral rolls are prepared under the authority of the Election Commission.\n3 — Correct: ECI recognises political parties and allots election symbols.\n4 — Incorrect: Election disputes are adjudicated through the judicial process, not finally decided by ECI.","revisionPoints":"Article 324: Superintendence, direction and control of elections.\nECI conducts elections to:\nLok Sabha\nState Legislative Assemblies\nRajya Sabha\nPresident\nVice-President\nElection disputes are ultimately handled through courts."},
{"no":36,"text":"Which of the following was a recommendation of the task force on direct taxes under Dr Vijay L. Kelkar in 2002?","A":"Abolition of Wealth Tax","B":"Increase in exemption limit of personal income to ₹1.20 lakh for widows","C":"Elimination of standard deduction","D":"Exemption from tax on dividends and capital gains from listed equity","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Kelkar Committee","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The Kelkar Task Force examined reforms aimed at simplifying India's direct-tax system.\nAbolition of Wealth Tax was among the recommendations identified by the source.","revisionPoints":"Kelkar Task Force: Important in India's tax reform history.\nFocus areas included:\nSimplification\nBroadening the tax base\nReducing distortions\nImproving compliance\nDistinguish direct taxes from indirect taxes."},
{"no":37,"text":"Match List I with List II:\nList I — Books\nList II — Authors\nA. My Presidential Years\n1. S. Radhakrishnan\nB. The Hindu View of Life\n2. V. V. Giri\nC. Voice of Conscience\n3. N. Sanjiva Reddy\nD. Without Fear or Favour\n4. R. Venkataraman\nCodes:","A":"2 1 4 3","B":"4 3 2 1","C":"2 3 4 1","D":"4 1 2 3","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Books & Authors","tags":["Presidential Memoirs","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"My Presidential Years → R. Venkataraman\nThe Hindu View of Life → S. Radhakrishnan\nVoice of Conscience → V. V. Giri\nWithout Fear or Favour → N. Sanjiva Reddy\nTherefore, the sequence is 4-1-2-3.","revisionPoints":"Radhakrishnan was a philosopher and former President.\nV. V. Giri served as President of India.\nR. Venkataraman served as President from 1987–1992.\nN. Sanjiva Reddy was India's sixth President."},
{"no":38,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nAdam Osborne produced the first portable computer.\nIan Wilmut created the first cloned sheep.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science History","tags":["Cloning","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Adam Osborne is associated with the Osborne 1, an early commercially available portable computer.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Ian Wilmut led the scientific team associated with Dolly, the first mammal cloned from an adult somatic cell.","revisionPoints":"Osborne 1: Important milestone in portable computing.\nDolly: Cloned sheep announced in 1997.\nSomatic cell nuclear transfer was used in Dolly's cloning.\nBiotechnology questions often connect scientists with landmark discoveries."},
{"no":39,"text":"Why did around five lakh people in Hong Kong demonstrate in the middle of 2003?","A":"They were demanding tax relief.","B":"They opposed the Hong Kong Government's plan to impose an internal security law.","C":"They were members of Falun Gong demanding religious freedom.","D":"They were demanding more direct elections in Hong Kong.","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Hong Kong","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies demands for greater direct electoral representation as the reason.\nThe 2003 demonstrations were part of broader political debates over Hong Kong's governance and democratic reforms.","revisionPoints":"Hong Kong operates under the principle of “One Country, Two Systems.”\nPolitical developments in Hong Kong involve:\nElectoral arrangements\nAutonomy\nRelationship with mainland China\n2003 was an important period of large-scale public demonstrations."},
{"no":40,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nNational Thermal Power Corporation has diversified into the hydropower sector.\nPower Grid Corporation of India has diversified into the telecom sector.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Sector Undertakings","tags":["NTPC","Power Grid","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: NTPC diversified beyond thermal generation into other power sectors including hydropower.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Power Grid Corporation expanded its infrastructure capabilities into telecommunications.\nHence both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"NTPC: Major Indian power-generation enterprise.\nPOWERGRID: Primarily responsible for electricity transmission infrastructure.\nPower-sector PSUs have historically diversified into related infrastructure and services.\nUPSC often tests the distinction between generation, transmission and distribution."},
{"no":41,"text":"The Ramon Magsaysay Award winner Shanta Sinha is known as:","A":"A campaigner for urban sanitation","B":"An anti-child labour activist","C":"An organiser of rainwater harvesting schemes","D":"An activist for the welfare of poor rural women","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Awards","tags":["Ramon Magsaysay Award","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Shanta Sinha is associated with the movement against child labour.\nShe worked for the rehabilitation and education of children withdrawn from labour.\nShe received the Ramon Magsaysay Award for her social work.","revisionPoints":"Ramon Magsaysay Award → major Asian award for leadership and public service.\nShanta Sinha → child labour abolition and education.\nChild labour is closely connected with poverty, lack of education and social exclusion."},
{"no":42,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nDamodar Valley Corporation is the first multipurpose river valley project of independent India.\nDamodar Valley Corporation includes thermal and gas power stations.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"River Valley Projects","tags":["Damodar Valley Corporation","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: DVC was established in 1948 and is India's first major multipurpose river valley project after Independence.\nStatement 2 — Correct: DVC's power-generation system includes thermal power and gas-based power facilities.\nIts functions extend beyond electricity generation.","revisionPoints":"DVC → Damodar River basin.\nEstablished in 1948.\nMajor objectives:\nFlood control\nIrrigation\nPower generation\nSoil conservation\nRegional development\nModelled partly on the Tennessee Valley Authority."},
{"no":43,"text":"Match List I with List II:\nList I – Person\nList II – Position\nA. B.P. Mishra\n1. Executive Director, IMF\nB. Suresh Kalamadi\n2. Chairman, SAIL\nC. Praful Patel\n3. President, Indian Olympic Association\nD. V.S. Jain\n4. Vice-President, South Asian Region, World Bank\nCodes:","A":"4 2 1 3","B":"1 3 4 2","C":"4 3 1 2","D":"1 2 4 3","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Personalities in News","tags":["Notable Personalities","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"A → 1: B.P. Mishra → Executive Director, IMF.\nB → 3: Suresh Kalmadi → President, Indian Olympic Association.\nC → 4: Praful Patel → Vice-President, South Asian Region, World Bank.\nD → 2: V.S. Jain → Chairman, SAIL.","revisionPoints":"IMF → international monetary cooperation and financial stability.\nSAIL → Steel Authority of India Limited.\nIndian Olympic Association → national Olympic movement in India.\nMatch-the-following questions often test institution–person associations."},
{"no":44,"text":"Who among the following is well known as an exponent of flute?","A":"Debu Choudhuri","B":"Madhup Mudgal","C":"Ronu Mazumdar","D":"Shafaat Ahmad","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["Hindustani Classical Music","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Ronu Majumdar is a well-known Indian flute player.\nHe is particularly associated with the bansuri tradition of Indian classical music.\nThe other names are associated with other instruments or vocal traditions.","revisionPoints":"Ronu Majumdar → Bansuri/flute.\nDebu Choudhuri → Sitar.\nMadhup Mudgal → Hindustani vocalist.\nShafaat Ahmed Khan → Tabla."},
{"no":45,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Indian States:\nAndhra Pradesh has the longest coastline.\nGujarat has the highest number of airports.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Indian States","tags":["Coastline","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source: Andhra Pradesh was identified as having the longest coastline among Indian States in the context of the question.\nStatement 2 — Correct according to the source: Gujarat had the highest number of airports among the States in the relevant period.\nThese questions are time-sensitive geographical facts, so the historical context of the paper matters.","revisionPoints":"Coastal States are important for:\nPorts\nFishing\nMaritime trade\nCoastal ecosystems\nGujarat has a significant Arabian Sea coastline.\nCoastline figures can change with measurement methodology and administrative changes."},
{"no":46,"text":"Tarun Bharat Sangh near Alwar is famous because of:","A":"Cultivation of genetically modified cotton","B":"Rehabilitation of women victims of AIDS","C":"Livelihood projects for destitute rural women","D":"Rainwater harvesting","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Water Conservation","tags":["Tarun Bharat Sangh","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Tarun Bharat Sangh is associated with community-based water conservation.\nIt became particularly known for reviving traditional water-harvesting structures in Rajasthan.\nThe organisation's work helped improve groundwater availability and local water security.","revisionPoints":"Tarun Bharat Sangh → water conservation.\nAssociated with Alwar, Rajasthan.\nTraditional structures such as johads are important in Rajasthan's water management.\nCommunity participation is central to sustainable water conservation."},
{"no":47,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe National Housing Bank, the apex institution of housing finance in India, was set up as a wholly owned subsidiary of the Reserve Bank of India.\nThe Small Industries Development Bank of India was established as a wholly owned subsidiary of the Industrial Development Bank of India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["NHB","SIDBI","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source key: NHB was established by an Act of Parliament and began operations in 1988; the source treats the statement about it being a wholly owned RBI subsidiary as incorrect.\nStatement 2 — Correct: SIDBI was established as a wholly owned subsidiary of IDBI.\nSIDBI focuses on financing and development of the micro, small and medium enterprise sector.","revisionPoints":"NHB → National Housing Bank.\nSIDBI → Small Industries Development Bank of India.\nSIDBI was established in 1990.\nSIDBI's core focus → MSME finance and development."},
{"no":48,"text":"Param Padma, which was in the news recently, is:","A":"A new Civilian Award instituted by the Government of India","B":"The name of a supercomputer developed by India","C":"The name given to a proposed network of canals linking northern and southern rivers of India","D":"A software programme to facilitate e-governance in Madhya Pradesh","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Computing","tags":["Param Padma","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Param Padma was associated with India's PARAM series of supercomputers.\nThe PARAM project was developed under the Centre for Development of Advanced Computing (C-DAC).\nIt represents India's development of indigenous high-performance computing capabilities.","revisionPoints":"PARAM → Indian supercomputing initiative.\nC-DAC → Centre for Development of Advanced Computing.\nSupercomputers are used for:\nWeather modelling\nScientific research\nSimulation\nDefence and engineering applications\nIndigenous computing capability is important for technological self-reliance."},
{"no":49,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nHema Malini is the Chairperson of the Children’s Film Society, India.\nYash Chopra is the Chairman of the Central Board of Film Certification of India.\nDilip Kumar, Raj Kapoor and Dev Anand have all been recipients of the Dadasaheb Phalke Award.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 3","B":"2 and 3","C":"2 only","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Cinema","tags":["Dada Saheb Phalke Award","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect according to the source.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Dilip Kumar, Raj Kapoor and Dev Anand were recipients of the Dadasaheb Phalke Award.","revisionPoints":"Dadasaheb Phalke Award → highest Indian award for lifetime contribution to cinema.\nNamed after Dhundiraj Govind Phalke.\nInstituted in 1969.\nIt is a lifetime achievement award, not an award for a single film."},
{"no":50,"text":"Famous golf player Vijay Singh is from which country?","A":"Fiji","B":"Mauritius","C":"Malaysia","D":"Kenya","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Golf","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Vijay Singh is a professional golfer from Fiji.\nHe became one of the prominent international golfers of his generation.\nHe won major professional golf tournaments, including the PGA Championship.","revisionPoints":"Vijay Singh → Fiji.\nFiji → island country in the South Pacific.\nCapital → Suva.\nFiji belongs to the broader Melanesian region of the Pacific."},
{"no":51,"text":"Latvia does share its borders with which one of the following countries?","A":"Russia","B":"Estonia","C":"Lithuania","D":"Poland","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Latvia","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Important source issue: The supplied 2004 source gives (d) Poland, but this is geographically incorrect.\nLatvia actually shares land borders with:\nEstonia\nRussia\nBelarus\nLithuania\nLatvia does not share a land border with Poland.","revisionPoints":"Latvia → Baltic State in Northern Europe.\nBaltic States:\nEstonia\nLatvia\nLithuania\nLatvia's neighbours → Estonia, Russia, Belarus, Lithuania.\nPoland lies south of Lithuania and does not directly border Latvia."},
{"no":52,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe highest deciding body for planning in India is the Planning Commission of India.\nThe Secretary of the Planning Commission of India is also the Secretary of National Development Council.\nThe Constitution includes economic and social planning in the Concurrent List in the Seventh Schedule.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"2 only","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Planning","tags":["Planning Commission","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: The Planning Commission was an executive body, not a constitutional body.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect according to the source.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Economic and social planning appears in the Concurrent List.\nTherefore, the source answer is 3 only.","revisionPoints":"Economic and social planning → Concurrent List.\nConcurrent List → both Union and States can legislate.\nPlanning Commission was established in 1950.\nPlanning Commission was replaced by NITI Aayog in 2015."},
{"no":53,"text":"Match the minerals with their locations:\nList I – Mineral\nList II – Location\nA. Coal\n1. Giridih\nB. Copper\n2. Jayamkondam\nC. Manganese\n3. Alwar\nD. Lignite\n4. Dharwar\nCodes:","A":"1 4 3 2","B":"2 3 4 1","C":"1 3 4 2","D":"2 4 3 1","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Minerals","tags":["Minerals of India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Coal → Giridih\nCopper → Dharwar\nManganese → Alwar\nLignite → Jayamkondam\nTherefore, the correct sequence is 1–4–3–2.","revisionPoints":"Giridih → coal resources.\nDharwar region → important mineral belt of Karnataka.\nJayamkondam → lignite deposits in Tamil Nadu.\nMineral distribution is strongly related to geological structure."},
{"no":54,"text":"Which among the following National Highway routes is the longest?","A":"Agra–Mumbai","B":"Chennai–Thane","C":"Kolkata–Hajira","D":"Pune–Machilipatnam","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Transport & Infrastructure","tags":["National Highways","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies Kolkata–Hajira as the longest route among the given options.\nIt represented a major east–west transportation corridor.\nHighway numbering and route descriptions have changed substantially after later national highway renumbering.","revisionPoints":"National Highways form India's major inter-state road network.\nThey connect:\nMajor cities\nPorts\nIndustrial centres\nState capitals\nBe careful with old vs new NH numbering in historical UPSC questions."},
{"no":55,"text":"Which of the following institutes have been recognised as Institutes of National Importance by an Act of Parliament?\nDakshina Bharat Hindi Prachar Sabha, Chennai\nNational Institute of Pharmaceutical Education and Research, Mohali\nSree Chitra Tirunal Institute for Medical Sciences and Technology, Thiruvananthapuram\nLakshmibai National Institute of Physical Education, Gwalior","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"3 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Institutions of National Importance","tags":["Institutes of National Importance","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The supplied source identifies 1, 2 and 3 as the correct combination.\nThese institutions received their status through parliamentary legislation.\nThe question tests knowledge of institutions established or recognised through specific Acts of Parliament.","revisionPoints":"Institute of National Importance (INI) status is conferred through legislation.\nSuch institutions generally have national-level importance in:\nEducation\nResearch\nProfessional training\nAlways distinguish INI status from ordinary autonomous or centrally funded institutions."},
{"no":56,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nBaking soda is used in fire extinguishers.\nQuicklime is used in the manufacture of glass.\nGypsum is used in the manufacture of Plaster of Paris.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Baking Soda","Gypsum","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Sodium bicarbonate is used in certain fire-extinguisher systems.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Lime compounds have applications in glass manufacture.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Gypsum is heated to produce Plaster of Paris.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Baking soda → sodium bicarbonate, NaHCO₃.\nQuicklime → calcium oxide, CaO.\nGypsum → calcium sulfate dihydrate.\nPlaster of Paris → calcium sulfate hemihydrate.\nHeating gypsum partially removes its water of crystallisation."},
{"no":57,"text":"With reference to the Indian Parliament, which one of the following is not correct?","A":"The Appropriation Bill must be passed by both Houses of Parliament before it can be enacted into law.","B":"No money shall be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund of India except under the appropriation made by the Appropriation Act.","C":"Finance Bill is required for proposing new taxes but no other Bill/Act is required for making changes in the rates of taxes which are already under operation.","D":"No Money Bill can be introduced except on the recommendation of the President.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Appropriation Bill","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The Appropriation Bill is a Money Bill.\nA Money Bill does not require approval of the Rajya Sabha in the same manner as an ordinary bill.\nThe Rajya Sabha can make recommendations, but the Lok Sabha has the final authority.\nTherefore, statement (a) is the incorrect statement according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Article 112 → Annual Financial Statement/Budget.\nArticle 114 → Appropriation Bills.\nMoney Bills can be introduced only in Lok Sabha.\nPrior recommendation of the President is required for introduction of a Money Bill.\nRajya Sabha has a limited role in Money Bills."},
{"no":58,"text":"Match the Sea with the Country:\nList I – Sea\nList II – Country\nA. Black Sea\n1. Bulgaria\nB. Red Sea\n2. China\nC. Yellow Sea\n3. Eritrea\nD. Caspian Sea\n4. Kazakhstan\nCodes:","A":"1 4 2 3","B":"2 3 1 4","C":"1 3 2 4","D":"2 4 1 3","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["World Seas","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Black Sea → Bulgaria\nRed Sea → Eritrea\nYellow Sea → China\nCaspian Sea → Kazakhstan\nTherefore, the sequence is 1–3–2–4.","revisionPoints":"Black Sea → bordered by countries including Bulgaria, Romania, Türkiye, Georgia, Russia and Ukraine.\nRed Sea → between northeastern Africa and the Arabian Peninsula.\nYellow Sea → between China and the Korean Peninsula.\nCaspian Sea → world's largest inland body of water."},
{"no":59,"text":"Which Article of the Constitution states that the executive power of every State shall be exercised so as not to impede or prejudice the exercise of the executive power of the Union?","A":"Article 257","B":"Article 258","C":"Article 355","D":"Article 356","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Centre-State Relations","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Article 257 deals with the control of the Union over States in certain cases.\nState executive power must not interfere with the exercise of Union executive power.\nIt reflects an important aspect of Union–State administrative relations.","revisionPoints":"Article 256 → obligation of States to comply with Union laws.\nArticle 257 → Union control over States in specified matters.\nArticle 258 → Union may confer powers/functions on States.\nArticle 355 → Union's duty to protect States.\nArticle 356 → President's Rule in case of constitutional breakdown."},
{"no":60,"text":"Which of the following pairs are correctly matched?\nPeriod\nWar\n1. AD 1767–69\nFirst Anglo-Maratha War\n2. AD 1790–92\nThird Mysore War\n3. AD 1824–26\nFirst Anglo-Burmese War\n4. AD 1845–46\nSecond Sikh War\nCodes:","A":"2 and 4","B":"3 and 4","C":"1 and 2","D":"2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Anglo-Indian Wars","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: 1767–69 was the First Anglo-Mysore War, not the First Anglo-Maratha War.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Third Anglo-Mysore War → 1790–1792.\nStatement 3 — Correct: First Anglo-Burmese War → 1824–1826.\nStatement 4 — Incorrect: 1845–46 was the First Anglo-Sikh War; the Second Anglo-Sikh War occurred in 1848–49.\nHence, 2 and 3 are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"First Anglo-Mysore War → 1767–69.\nFirst Anglo-Maratha War → 1775–82.\nThird Anglo-Mysore War → 1790–92.\nFirst Anglo-Burmese War → 1824–26.\nFirst Anglo-Sikh War → 1845–46.\nSecond Anglo-Sikh War → 1848–49."},
{"no":61,"text":"Consider the following:\nMahadeo Hills\nSahyadri Parvat\nSatpura Range\nWhat is the correct sequence of the above from north to south?","A":"1–2–3","B":"2–1–3","C":"1–3–2","D":"2–3–1","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Central Highlands","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Mahadeo Hills are part of the Satpura system.\nSatpura Range lies north of the Sahyadri/Western Ghats.\nTherefore, from north to south:\nMahadeo Hills\nSatpura Range\nSahyadri Parvat","revisionPoints":"Satpura Range → central India.\nSahyadri → Western Ghats.\nWestern Ghats run roughly parallel to India's western coast.\nQuestions on ranges often require understanding their relative geographical position."},
{"no":62,"text":"Lake Sambhar is nearest to which one of the following cities of Rajasthan?","A":"Bharatpur","B":"Jaipur","C":"Jodhpur","D":"Udaipur","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Sambhar Lake","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Sambhar Lake is located in Rajasthan, southwest of Jaipur.\nIt is India's largest inland salt lake.\nIts proximity to Jaipur makes Jaipur the nearest option.","revisionPoints":"Sambhar Lake → Rajasthan.\nIndia's largest inland salt lake.\nImportant for:\nSalt production\nMigratory birds\nWetland ecology\nLocated in the Sambhar basin."},
{"no":63,"text":"Match the Articles of the Constitution with their provisions:\nList I – Article\nList II – Provision\nA. Article 14\n1. No discrimination on specified grounds\nB. Article 15\n2. Equality before law\nC. Article 16\n3. Abolition of untouchability\nD. Article 17\n4. Equality of opportunity in public employment\nCodes:","A":"2–4–1–3","B":"3–1–4–2","C":"2–1–4–3","D":"3–4–1–2","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right to Equality","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Article 14 → 2: Equality before law and equal protection of laws.\nArticle 15 → 1: Prohibition of discrimination on specified grounds.\nArticle 16 → 4: Equality of opportunity in public employment.\nArticle 17 → 3: Abolition of untouchability.","revisionPoints":"Article 14 → Equality before law.\nArticle 15 → Prohibition of discrimination.\nArticle 16 → Equality in public employment.\nArticle 17 → Abolition of untouchability.\nThese are part of Fundamental Rights."},
{"no":64,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Cirrus clouds are composed of ice crystals.","B":"Cirrus clouds exhibit a flat base and have the appearance of rising domes.","C":"Cumulus clouds are white and thin and have a fibrous and feathery appearance.","D":"Cumulus clouds are classified as high clouds.","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Clouds","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Cirrus clouds occur at high altitudes.\nBecause of the very low temperatures at those heights, they consist mainly of ice crystals.\nThey typically have a thin, wispy or feather-like appearance.\nCumulus clouds are generally associated with heap-like formations, not high-level cirrus characteristics.","revisionPoints":"Cirrus → high, thin, ice-crystal clouds.\nCumulus → heap/puffy clouds.\nStratus → layered clouds.\nCloud classification depends mainly on altitude and form."},
{"no":65,"text":"Match the National Parks/Sanctuaries with their States:\nList I\nList II\nA. Kanger Ghati National Park\n1. Chhattisgarh\nB. Nagarhole National Park\n2. Haryana\nC. Kugti Wildlife Sanctuary\n3. Himachal Pradesh\nD. Sultanpur Bird Sanctuary\n4. Karnataka\nCodes:","A":"3–2–1–4","B":"1–4–3–2","C":"3–4–1–2","D":"1–2–3–4","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"National Parks & Sanctuaries","tags":["National Parks","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Kanger Ghati → Chhattisgarh\nNagarhole → Karnataka\nKugti Wildlife Sanctuary → Himachal Pradesh\nSultanpur Bird Sanctuary → Haryana","revisionPoints":"Kanger Ghati → Chhattisgarh.\nNagarhole → Karnataka.\nKugti → Himachal Pradesh.\nSultanpur → Haryana.\nProtected-area questions frequently test location + ecosystem/species."},
{"no":66,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Gulfs with narrow fronts and wider rears experience high tides.","B":"Tidal currents take place when a gulf is connected with the open sea by a narrow channel.","C":"Tidal bore occurs when a tide enters the narrow and shallow estuary of a river.","D":"The tidal nature of the mouth of the Hooghly is of crucial importance to Kolkata as a port.","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Oceanography","tags":["Tides","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies (b) as correct.\nNarrow connections between enclosed water bodies and the open sea can produce strong tidal currents.\nThe movement of water caused by tides can become particularly pronounced through narrow channels.","revisionPoints":"Tidal current → horizontal movement of water due to tides.\nTidal bore → surge/wave moving upstream in certain estuaries.\nTidal phenomena depend on:\nBasin shape\nCoastal geometry\nTidal range\nChannel width and depth"},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Islamic Calendar is twelve days shorter than the Gregorian Calendar.\nThe Islamic Calendar began in AD 632.\nThe Gregorian Calendar is a solar calendar.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"General Knowledge","tags":["Calendars","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: The Islamic lunar year is approximately 10–12 days shorter than the Gregorian solar year; the statement's fixed wording is therefore not accepted by the source.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The Islamic calendar's era begins with the Hijra, conventionally dated to AD 622, not AD 632.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The Gregorian calendar is a solar calendar.","revisionPoints":"Islamic/Hijri calendar → lunar calendar.\nHijra → AD 622.\nGregorian calendar → solar calendar.\nLunar years are shorter than solar years, so Islamic months shift through the seasons."},
{"no":68,"text":"Assertion (A): Bangalore receives much higher average annual rainfall than that of Mangalore.\nReason (R): Bangalore has the benefit of receiving rainfall both from southwest and northeast monsoons.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Monsoon","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source marks both the Assertion and Reason as correct.\nThe question connects rainfall distribution with the monsoon systems affecting southern India.\nRainfall varies significantly according to relief, wind direction and monsoon exposure.","revisionPoints":"Southwest Monsoon → June–September.\nNortheast/retreating monsoon → especially important for Tamil Nadu and parts of southeast India.\nWestern Ghats strongly influence rainfall through orographic effects.\nRainfall distribution is not uniform across peninsular India."},
{"no":69,"text":"Assertion (A): The Central Rural Sanitation Programme was launched in 1986 to improve the quality of life of rural people in India.\nReason (R): Rural sanitation is a subject in the Concurrent List in the Constitution of India.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Rural Sanitation","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: The Central Rural Sanitation Programme was launched in 1986.\nReason — Incorrect: Rural sanitation is not placed in the Concurrent List in the manner stated by the question.\nTherefore, the Reason cannot explain the Assertion.","revisionPoints":"Rural sanitation → important component of public health and rural development.\nSanitation programmes aim to improve:\nHygiene\nPublic health\nEnvironmental conditions\nQuality of life\nConcurrent List allows both Union and State legislative action."},
{"no":70,"text":"Assertion (A): West-flowing rivers of Peninsular India have no deltas.\nReason (R): These rivers do not carry any alluvial sediments.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Peninsular Rivers","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct according to the source: Major west-flowing peninsular rivers generally form estuaries rather than large deltas.\nReason — Incorrect: These rivers do carry sediments.\nTheir tendency to form estuaries is related to factors such as:\nSteeper gradients\nShorter courses\nNarrow coastal plains\nCoastal conditions","revisionPoints":"East-flowing rivers → many form deltas.\nWest-flowing rivers → commonly form estuaries.\nImportant west-flowing rivers:\nNarmada\nTapi\nMahi\nEstuary → tidal mouth where freshwater mixes with seawater."},
{"no":71,"text":"Assertion (A): The thickness of the atmosphere is maximum over the Equator.\nReason (R): High insolation and strong convection currents occur over the Equator.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Atmosphere","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Assertion — False: The atmosphere is not uniformly distributed in thickness.\nReason — Correct: Strong solar heating near the Equator produces strong convection.\nWarm air rises in equatorial regions, contributing to a greater vertical extent of the atmosphere there, but the source does not consider this equivalent to maximum atmospheric thickness in the Assertion.","revisionPoints":"Equator receives strong insolation.\nStrong heating → rising air → convection.\nAtmospheric pressure and density vary with:\nTemperature\nAltitude\nLatitude\nThe troposphere is generally deeper over the Equator than near the poles."},
{"no":72,"text":"Assertion (A): In our houses, the current in AC electricity line changes direction 60 times per second.\nReason (R): The frequency of alternating voltage supplied is 60 hertz.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["AC Current","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The supplied 2004 source uses 60 Hz for the domestic AC supply in this question.\nFrequency means the number of complete cycles per second.\nIn one AC cycle, the current reverses direction twice.","revisionPoints":"Frequency → cycles per second.\nUnit → hertz (Hz).\nAC periodically changes direction.\nSource-specific note: The question reflects its historical/source framing; India's standard domestic AC frequency today is generally 50 Hz."},
{"no":73,"text":"Assertion (A): Fatty acids should be a part of the balanced human diet.\nReason (R): The cells of the human body cannot synthesize any fatty acids.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Fatty Acids","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Fats and fatty acids are important components of a balanced diet.\nThey provide energy and participate in cell structure and biological processes.\nReason — Incorrect: The human body can synthesize several fatty acids.\nHowever, some essential fatty acids must be obtained through the diet.","revisionPoints":"Fatty acids are components of lipids.\nFunctions of dietary fats:\nEnergy source\nCell membranes\nAbsorption of fat-soluble vitamins\nEssential fatty acids include linoleic acid and alpha-linolenic acid.\nEssential nutrients cannot be produced sufficiently by the body."},
{"no":74,"text":"Assertion (A): India does not export natural rubber.\nReason (R): About 97% of India's demand for natural rubber is met from domestic production.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Natural Rubber","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Assertion — False: India can export natural rubber.\nReason — Correct according to the source: Domestic production meets a very large share of India's requirement.\nHigh domestic production does not mean that exports are completely absent.","revisionPoints":"India is an important producer and consumer of natural rubber.\nMajor rubber-growing region → Kerala.\nRubber cultivation requires:\nWarm climate\nHigh rainfall\nWell-drained soil\nDomestic production and international trade can coexist."},
{"no":75,"text":"Assertion (A): For the first time, India had no trade deficit in the year 2002–2003.\nReason (R): For the first time, India's exports crossed US$50 billion in the year 2002–03.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["Trade Deficit","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Assertion — Incorrect according to the source: India did not have zero trade deficit in 2002–03.\nReason — Correct according to the source: Exports crossed the US$50 billion level.\nCrossing a particular export threshold does not automatically eliminate the trade deficit, because imports must also be considered.","revisionPoints":"Trade balance = Exports − Imports.\nExports > imports → trade surplus.\nImports > exports → trade deficit.\nExport growth alone does not determine the trade balance."},
{"no":76,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe loans disbursed to farmers under the Kisan Credit Card Scheme are covered under Rashtriya Krishi Beema Yojana of Life Insurance Corporation of India.\nKisan Credit Card holders are provided personal accident insurance of ₹50,000 for accidental death and ₹25,000 for permanent disability.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Kisan Credit Card","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies Statement 1 as correct.\nStatement 2 is not accepted by the source.\nKisan Credit Card was designed to provide farmers with timely access to short-term credit for agricultural activities.","revisionPoints":"Kisan Credit Card (KCC) → agricultural credit facility.\nIntended to support:\nCrop cultivation\nInput purchases\nWorking-capital requirements\nCrop insurance and credit-linked insurance are distinct concepts."},
{"no":77,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nRegarding procurement of food grains, Government of India follows a procurement target rather than an open-ended procurement policy.\nGovernment of India announces minimum support prices only for cereals.\nUnder TPDS, wheat and rice are issued by the Government of India at uniform Central Issue Prices to States/UTs.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Minimum Support Price","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: The source describes procurement in terms of a target rather than an unrestricted/open-ended policy.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: MSP is announced for various agricultural commodities, not only cereals.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Wheat and rice were issued under TPDS at Central Issue Prices.","revisionPoints":"MSP → Minimum Support Price.\nTPDS → Targeted Public Distribution System.\nFood procurement helps maintain:\nFood security\nBuffer stocks\nPrice support\nFCI plays a major role in procurement, storage and distribution."},
{"no":78,"text":"India continues to be dependent on imports to meet the requirement of oilseeds because:\nFarmers prefer to grow food grains with highly remunerative support prices.\nMost cultivation of oilseed crops continues to depend on rainfall.\nOils from seeds of tree origin and rice bran have remained unexploited.\nIt is far cheaper to import oilseeds than to cultivate oilseed crops.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Oilseeds","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Farmers may favour crops with stronger price-support incentives.\nStatement 2 — Correct: A significant portion of oilseed cultivation has historically depended on rainfall.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect according to the source.\nStatement 4 — Incorrect: Import cost alone is not the principal explanation given for India's oilseed dependence.","revisionPoints":"Oilseeds → source of edible oils.\nMajor oilseed crops include:\nGroundnut\nRapeseed-mustard\nSoybean\nSunflower\nRainfed agriculture is vulnerable to monsoon variability.\nIndia's edible-oil demand has historically involved both domestic production and imports."},
{"no":79,"text":"The world's longest-ruling head of government is from:","A":"Switzerland","B":"Cuba","C":"Zimbabwe","D":"New Zealand","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Cuba","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The question reflects the historical context of 2004.\nThe source identifies Cuba.\nSuch questions about political leaders are highly time-sensitive, so the answer must be understood in the context of the year in which the paper was conducted.","revisionPoints":"Cuba → Caribbean island country.\nCapital → Havana.\nCuba is located in the Caribbean Sea.\nHistorical current-affairs questions should always be interpreted according to the relevant year."},
{"no":80,"text":"Liberia was in the international news in recent times for:","A":"Harbouring terrorists associated with religious fundamentalism","B":"Supplying raw uranium to North Korea","C":"Its long-running civil war killing or displacing thousands of people","D":"Cultivation of drug-yielding crops and smuggling of drugs","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Liberia","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Liberia experienced prolonged civil conflict.\nThe conflict resulted in large-scale:\nDeaths\nDisplacement\nHumanitarian crises\nDestruction of infrastructure\nThe question reflects the situation reported around the time of the 2004 examination.","revisionPoints":"Liberia → West Africa.\nCapital → Monrovia.\nLocated on the Atlantic coast.\nLiberia experienced two major civil wars:\nFirst Civil War → 1989–1997\nSecond Civil War → 1999–2003\nThe region's conflicts had major humanitarian and refugee consequences."},
{"no":81,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nP. V. Narasimha Rao’s government established diplomatic relations between India and Israel.\nAriel Sharon was the second Prime Minister of Israel to have visited India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"India's Foreign Relations","tags":["India-Israel Relations","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: India established full diplomatic relations with Israel in 1992, during P. V. Narasimha Rao's government.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The source does not accept the statement that Ariel Sharon was the second Israeli Prime Minister to visit India.","revisionPoints":"India–Israel full diplomatic relations → 1992.\nP. V. Narasimha Rao → Prime Minister, 1991–1996.\nIndia and Israel cooperate in:\nDefence\nAgriculture\nScience and technology\nWater management"},
{"no":82,"text":"The reason for Chechnya to be in the news recently was:","A":"Discovery of huge reserves of oil","B":"Separatist rebellious activities by the local people","C":"Continuous conflict between government troops and the narcotic mafia","D":"Intense cold wave killing hundreds of people","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Chechnya","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Chechnya is a region of the Russian Federation in the North Caucasus.\nIt became internationally prominent because of prolonged separatist conflict and armed violence.\nThe conflict involved Chechen separatists and Russian federal forces.","revisionPoints":"Chechnya → North Caucasus.\nPart of the Russian Federation.\nCaucasus region lies between the Black Sea and Caspian Sea.\nImportant neighbouring areas include Russia, Georgia and Azerbaijan."},
{"no":83,"text":"The Prime Minister of which one of the following countries was assassinated in 2003?","A":"Czech Republic","B":"Romania","C":"Serbia","D":"Slovenia","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Serbia","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Zoran Đinđić, Prime Minister of Serbia, was assassinated in March 2003.\nHis assassination was a major political event in post-Milošević Serbia.","revisionPoints":"Zoran Đinđić → Prime Minister of Serbia.\nAssassinated → 2003.\nSerbia → Southeast Europe / Balkans.\nBelgrade → capital of Serbia."},
{"no":84,"text":"Consider the following companies:\nVoltas\nTitan Industries\nRallis India\nIndian Hotels\nWhich of the above companies are in the Tata Group?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Sector","tags":["Tata Group","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Voltas → Tata Group company.\nTitan Industries → Tata Group company.\nRallis India → Tata Group company.\nIndian Hotels → Tata Group company.\nTherefore, all four belong to the Tata Group in the context of the question.","revisionPoints":"Tata Group → major Indian business group.\nVoltas → engineering, cooling and air-conditioning.\nTitan → jewellery, watches and lifestyle products.\nRallis India → agricultural inputs.\nIndian Hotels → hospitality sector."},
{"no":85,"text":"In which country did ethnic violence between the Hema and Lendu communities result in the death of hundreds of people?","A":"Democratic Republic of Congo","B":"Indonesia","C":"Nigeria","D":"Zambia","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Democratic Republic of Congo","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The Hema–Lendu conflict occurred in the Ituri region of the Democratic Republic of Congo.\nIt involved severe ethnic violence and resulted in large-scale loss of life and displacement.","revisionPoints":"DR Congo → Central Africa.\nCapital → Kinshasa.\nIturi → northeastern DR Congo.\nThe Great Lakes region of Africa has experienced several complex ethnic and political conflicts."},
{"no":86,"text":"More than 40 Heads of State/Governments were invited by Vladimir Putin in May 2003 to:","A":"Discuss issues related to the rehabilitation of Iraq","B":"Celebrate the tricentenary of the city of St Petersburg","C":"Convene a meeting regarding a missile shield for European and CIS countries","D":"Develop strategies for containing global terrorism","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Russia","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"St Petersburg was founded in 1703 by Peter the Great.\nIts 300th anniversary was celebrated in 2003.\nThe event attracted numerous international leaders.","revisionPoints":"St Petersburg → major Russian city.\nFounded → 1703.\nFounded by → Peter the Great.\nFormerly known as:\nPetrograd\nLeningrad\nIt is located on the Neva River, near the Baltic Sea."},
{"no":87,"text":"Consider the following geological phenomena:\nDevelopment of a fault\nMovement along a fault\nImpact produced by a volcanic eruption\nFolding of rocks\nWhich of the above can cause earthquakes?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Earthquakes","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Fault development can generate stresses and earthquakes.\nMovement along faults is a major cause of tectonic earthquakes.\nVolcanic activity can produce earthquakes.\nFolding of rocks results from tectonic forces and can also generate seismic activity.\nHence, the source identifies all four.","revisionPoints":"Major earthquake causes:\nTectonic movement\nFaulting\nVolcanic activity\nCrustal deformation\nFocus/Hypocentre → point inside Earth where an earthquake originates.\nEpicentre → point on the surface directly above the focus.\nSeismic waves carry earthquake energy."},
{"no":88,"text":"Which Article of the Constitution states:\n“No child below the age of fourteen years shall be employed to work in any factory or mine or engaged in any other hazardous employment”?","A":"Article 24","B":"Article 45","C":"Article 330","D":"Article 368","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Child Labour","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Article 24 prohibits employment of children below 14 years in factories, mines and other hazardous employment.\nIt is part of the Fundamental Rights dealing with protection against exploitation.","revisionPoints":"Article 23 → prohibition of trafficking and forced labour.\nArticle 24 → prohibition of child labour in hazardous employment.\nArticle 21A → Right to Education for children aged 6–14 years.\nFundamental Rights are primarily enforceable through constitutional remedies."},
{"no":89,"text":"According to the National Human Rights Commission Act, 1993, who can be its Chairman?","A":"Any serving Judge of the Supreme Court","B":"Any serving Judge of the High Court","C":"Only a retired Chief Justice of India","D":"Only a retired Chief Justice of High Court","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Human Rights","tags":["National Human Rights Commission","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Under the legal framework applicable when the question was asked, the NHRC Chairperson was required to have been a former Chief Justice of India.\nThe question therefore tests knowledge of the statutory composition of the NHRC.","revisionPoints":"NHRC → National Human Rights Commission.\nEstablished under the Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993.\nFunctions include:\nInquiry into human-rights violations\nReview of safeguards\nHuman-rights awareness\nNHRC is a statutory body, not a constitutional body."},
{"no":90,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is correctly matched?","A":"Pitt’s India Act — Warren Hastings","B":"Doctrine of Lapse — Dalhousie","C":"Vernacular Press Act — Curzon","D":"Ilbert Bill — Ripon","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Doctrine of Lapse","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The supplied source gives (c) as its answer.\nHowever, this is a source-key issue:\nVernacular Press Act → Lord Lytton, 1878.\nIlbert Bill → associated with Lord Ripon, 1883.\nDoctrine of Lapse → associated with Lord Dalhousie.\nThus, the source's answer key should be treated cautiously.","revisionPoints":"Pitt's India Act → 1784.\nDoctrine of Lapse → Lord Dalhousie.\nVernacular Press Act → Lord Lytton, 1878.\nIlbert Bill → Lord Ripon, 1883.\nAlways distinguish Governor-General/Viceroy + legislation/policy."},
{"no":91,"text":"Match the Institutes with their locations:\nList I – Institute\nList II – Location\nA. Indian Institute of Geomagnetism\n1. Coimbatore\nB. International Research Centre for Powder Metallurgy and New Materials\n2. Mumbai\nC. Salim Ali Centre for Ornithology and Natural History\n3. Jabalpur\nD. Tropical Forestry Research Institute\n4. Hyderabad\nCodes:","A":"2–3–1–4","B":"1–4–2–3","C":"2–4–1–3","D":"1–3–2–4","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Research Institutions","tags":["Research Institutes","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Indian Institute of Geomagnetism → Mumbai\nInternational Research Centre for Powder Metallurgy and New Materials → Hyderabad\nSalim Ali Centre for Ornithology and Natural History → Coimbatore\nTropical Forestry Research Institute → Jabalpur","revisionPoints":"Geomagnetism → study of Earth's magnetic field.\nSalim Ali Centre → ornithology and natural-history research.\nTropical Forestry Research Institute → forestry research.\nInstitutional-location questions require remembering specialised research centres."},
{"no":92,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIndira Gandhi Centre for Atomic Research uses fast reactor technology.\nAtomic Minerals Directorate for Research and Exploration is engaged in heavy-water production.\nIndian Rare Earths Limited is engaged in the manufacture of zircon for India's Nuclear Programme besides other rare-earth products.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2","C":"1 and 3","D":"2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Energy","tags":["Nuclear Research","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: IGCAR is associated with fast reactor technology.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: Atomic Minerals Directorate focuses on exploration and evaluation of atomic mineral resources, not heavy-water production.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Indian Rare Earths Limited is associated with rare-earth and mineral products including zircon.","revisionPoints":"IGCAR → Kalpakkam, Tamil Nadu.\nFocus → fast breeder reactor technology and related research.\nAMD → exploration of atomic minerals.\nIREL → mineral sands and rare-earth-related products."},
{"no":93,"text":"Which of the following pairs is correctly matched?","A":"Reinhold Messner — Computer Technology","B":"Harlow Shapley — Astronomy","C":"Gregor Mendel — Hereditary Theory","D":"Godfrey Hounsfield — CT Scan","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science Personalities","tags":["Scientists","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The supplied source gives (a) as its answer.\nHowever, the source contains a clear factual mismatch:\nReinhold Messner → mountaineer.\nHarlow Shapley → astronomer.\nGregor Mendel → foundational work on heredity/genetics.\nGodfrey Hounsfield → inventor/developer associated with CT scanning.\nTherefore, the source answer should not be treated as conceptually correct.","revisionPoints":"Reinhold Messner → mountaineering.\nHarlow Shapley → astronomy; Milky Way structure.\nGregor Mendel → genetics and heredity.\nGodfrey Hounsfield → CT scan technology.\nThis is an example where the provided source/key contains an error."},
{"no":94,"text":"Consider the following international languages:\nArabic\nFrench\nSpanish\nThe correct sequence in decreasing order of number of speakers is:","A":"3–1–2","B":"1–3–2","C":"3–2–1","D":"1–2–3","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"General Knowledge","tags":["World Languages","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source gives Spanish → French → Arabic as the sequence.\nThis question reflects speaker estimates and classification practices relevant to the period of the 2004 examination.\nCounts can vary depending on whether native speakers or total speakers are considered.","revisionPoints":"Spanish → major language of Spain and Latin America.\nFrench → major international language across Europe, Africa and elsewhere.\nArabic → major language of West Asia and North Africa.\nLanguage population rankings depend on the definition of “speaker.”"},
{"no":95,"text":"Who among the following was never the Lok Sabha Speaker?","A":"K. V. K. Sundaram","B":"G. S. Dhillon","C":"Baliram Bhagat","D":"Hukum Singh","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Lok Sabha Speaker","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies K. V. K. Sundaram as the person who was never Lok Sabha Speaker.\nThe other names were associated with the office of Speaker during different periods.","revisionPoints":"Lok Sabha Speaker → presiding officer of the House.\nElected by the Lok Sabha.\nPresides over parliamentary proceedings.\nDecides whether a bill is a Money Bill for constitutional purposes."},
{"no":96,"text":"Two cars X and Y start from places A and B respectively, which are 700 km apart, at 9 a.m.\nBoth travel at an average speed of 60 km/hr.\nCar X stops at 10 a.m. and starts again at 11 a.m.\nCar Y continues without stopping.\nWhen do the two cars cross each other?","A":"2:40 p.m.","B":"3:20 p.m.","C":"4:10 p.m.","D":"4:20 p.m.","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"From 9–10 a.m., X travels:\n1 × 60 = 60 km\nX stops for one hour.\nFrom 9 a.m. onward, Y continuously travels.\nThe combined effective distance covered after accounting for X's stop gives the meeting time of 3:20 p.m.\nHence the source answer is (b).","revisionPoints":"Relative speed when two objects move towards each other:\nSum of their speeds\nWhen one object stops:\nRelative motion changes during the stopping interval.\nAlways divide the problem into time intervals."},
{"no":97,"text":"In a test, there are five items under List A and five under List B. Examinees must match each List A item with the corresponding List B item.\nIt is given that:\nNo examinee has given the correct answer.\nNo two examinees have identical answers.\nWhat is the maximum number of examinees who could take the test?","A":"24","B":"26","C":"119","D":"129","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Five items can be matched in:\n5! = 120 possible ways.\nExactly one of these is the completely correct matching.\nSince no examinee gives the correct answer, that one arrangement must be excluded.\nMaximum possible examinees:\n120 − 1 = 119","revisionPoints":"Number of permutations of n distinct objects → n!\nFor 5 objects:\n5! = 120\nExcluding one prohibited arrangement:\n120 − 1 = 119\nThis is a basic permutation/counting application."},
{"no":98,"text":"The magazine on science is published by:","A":"Aryan Publishers","B":"Bharat Publishers","C":"Charan Publishers","D":"Dev Publishers","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"From the conditions:\nPolitics → Aryan Publishers.\nFilms → edited by Swami and not published by Charan.\nScience → edited by Ila.\nDev Publishers → magazine edited by Feroz.\nApplying the remaining constraints gives:\nScience → Charan Publishers.","revisionPoints":"This is a logical arrangement problem.\nUse definite conditions first.\nEliminate impossible combinations systematically.\nIn matching puzzles, avoid making an assumption until all constraints are considered."},
{"no":99,"text":"The magazine on sports is:","A":"Edited by Feroz","B":"Edited by Gurbaksh","C":"Published by Bharat Publishers","D":"Published by Charan Publishers","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Dev Publishers published the magazine edited by Feroz.\nScience is edited by Ila.\nFilms are edited by Swami.\nTherefore, the remaining editor Feroz is associated with the sports magazine.","revisionPoints":"In a one-to-one matching puzzle:\nEach editor corresponds to one magazine.\nEach publisher corresponds to one magazine.\nOnce three editor–subject relationships are fixed, the remaining relationship can be derived by elimination."},
{"no":100,"text":"The magazine on films is:","A":"Published by Dev Publishers","B":"Published by Bharat Publishers","C":"Edited by Gurbaksh","D":"Published by Charan Publishers","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The conditions establish:\nFilms → edited by Swami.\nFilms → not published by Charan.\nDev → magazine edited by Feroz.\nSports → edited by Feroz.\nTherefore, Dev cannot publish the films magazine.\nCombining the remaining possibilities gives Bharat Publishers.","revisionPoints":"Logical arrangement questions often require:\nDirect clues\nNegative clues\nElimination\nOne-to-one matching\nA negative condition such as “not published by” can be as useful as a positive condition.\nSolve these puzzles by constructing a small subject–editor–publisher matrix."},
{"no":101,"text":"The magazine on politics is:","A":"Edited by Ila","B":"Edited by Gurbaksh","C":"Published by Dev Publishers","D":"Published by Charan Publishers","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"From the conditions:\nScience → edited by Ila.\nFilms → edited by Swami.\nSports → edited by Feroz.\nTherefore, the remaining editor Gurbaksh must edit the politics magazine.","revisionPoints":"This completes the editor–subject matching puzzle.\nKnown editors:\nScience → Ila\nFilms → Swami\nSports → Feroz\nPolitics → Gurbaksh\nUse elimination only after applying all definite clues."},
{"no":102,"text":"Match the distinguished ladies with their areas of work:\nList I – Person\nList II – Area\nA. Jhumpa Lahiri\n1. Science and environment\nB. Sunita Narain\n2. Novel writing\nC. Naina Lal Kidwai\n3. Film industry\nD. Ravina Raj Kohli\n4. Banking\n5. Television media\nCodes:","A":"4–5–3–1","B":"2–1–4–5","C":"4–1–3–5","D":"2–5–4–1","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Personalities in News","tags":["Notable Women","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Jhumpa Lahiri → Novel writing\nSunita Narain → Science and environment\nNaina Lal Kidwai → Banking\nRavina Raj Kohli → Television media","revisionPoints":"Jhumpa Lahiri → fiction/novel writing.\nSunita Narain → environmental work and advocacy.\nNaina Lal Kidwai → banking and financial sector.\nRavina Raj Kohli → television/media.\nMatch-the-following questions often combine current affairs with personalities."},
{"no":103,"text":"Which one of the following does not border Panama?","A":"Costa Rica","B":"Pacific Ocean","C":"Colombia","D":"Venezuela","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Panama","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Panama has land borders with:\nCosta Rica to the west.\nColombia to the southeast.\nIt has coastlines on:\nCaribbean Sea\nPacific Ocean\nVenezuela does not share a border with Panama.","revisionPoints":"Panama → Central America.\nBorders → Costa Rica and Colombia.\nPanama Canal connects the Atlantic and Pacific oceans.\nPanama is an important geographical link between North and South America."},
{"no":104,"text":"A and B start from the same point and in the same direction at 7 a.m. to walk around a rectangular field of 400 m × 300 m.\nA walks at 3 km/hr.\nB walks at 2.5 km/hr.\nHow many times will they cross each other if they continue walking until 12:30 p.m.?","A":"Not even once","B":"Once","C":"Twice","D":"Thrice","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Perimeter of the rectangular field:\n2 × (400 + 300) = 1400 m\nRelative speed:\n3 − 2.5 = 0.5 km/hr\nRelative distance covered in 5.5 hours:\n0.5 × 5.5 = 2.75 km\nThe source identifies the answer as not even once, considering the required meeting condition on the circular track.","revisionPoints":"Rectangular perimeter:\n2(l + b)\nSame-direction relative speed:\nDifference of speeds\nOpposite-direction relative speed:\nSum of speeds\nCircular-track problems require comparing relative distance with track circumference."},
{"no":105,"text":"Match the beaches in India with their States:\nList I – Beach\nList II – State\nA. Gopnath Beach\n1. Andhra Pradesh\nB. Lawsons Bay Beach\n2. Kerala\nC. Devbagh Beach\n3. Gujarat\nD. Sinquerim Beach\n4. Goa\n5. Karnataka\nCodes:","A":"5–4–2–1","B":"3–1–5–4","C":"5–1–2–4","D":"3–4–5–1","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Beaches of India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Gopnath Beach → Gujarat\nLawsons Bay Beach → Andhra Pradesh\nDevbagh Beach → Karnataka\nSinquerim Beach → Goa","revisionPoints":"Gopnath → Gujarat coast.\nLawsons Bay → Visakhapatnam region, Andhra Pradesh.\nDevbagh → Karnataka coast.\nSinquerim → Goa.\nIndian coastal geography includes Arabian Sea, Bay of Bengal and Indian Ocean environments."},
{"no":106,"text":"A car is running on a road at a uniform speed of 60 km/hr. The net resultant force on the car is:","A":"Driving force in the direction of car's motion","B":"Resistance force opposite to the direction of car's motion","C":"An inclined force","D":"Equal to zero","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Newton's Laws","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The car is moving at uniform speed in a straight path.\nTherefore, its acceleration is zero.\nAccording to Newton's second law:\nF = ma\nIf a = 0 → net force = 0.\nThe driving force and resistive forces balance each other.","revisionPoints":"Uniform velocity → zero acceleration.\nNet force:\nF = ma\nZero net force does not mean that individual forces are absent.\nIt means the forces are balanced."},
{"no":107,"text":"Match the Biosphere Reserves with the States:\nList I – Biosphere Reserve\nList II – State\nA. Similipal\n1. Sikkim\nB. Dehong Deband\n2. Uttaranchal\nC. Nokrek\n3. Arunachal Pradesh\nD. Kanchenjunga\n4. Orissa\n5. Meghalaya\nCodes:","A":"1–3–5–4","B":"4–5–2–1","C":"1–5–2–4","D":"4–3–5–1","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biosphere Reserves","tags":["Biosphere Reserve","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Similipal → Odisha (Orissa)\nDehong-Debang → Arunachal Pradesh\nNokrek → Meghalaya\nKanchenjunga → Sikkim","revisionPoints":"Similipal → Odisha.\nDihang-Dibang/Dehong-Debang → Arunachal Pradesh.\nNokrek → Meghalaya.\nKanchenjunga → Sikkim.\nBiosphere reserves aim at conservation of ecosystems, biodiversity and genetic resources."},
{"no":108,"text":"The research work of Paul Lauterbur and Peter Mansfield, Nobel Prize winners for Medicine in 2003, relates to:","A":"Control of AIDS","B":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging","C":"Respiratory diseases","D":"Genetic engineering","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Medical Science","tags":["Nobel Prize","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Paul Lauterbur and Peter Mansfield made major contributions to the development of Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI).\nMRI uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed images of internal body structures.\nIt does not use ionising X-rays like conventional CT imaging.","revisionPoints":"MRI → Magnetic Resonance Imaging.\nUses:\nStrong magnetic field\nRadiofrequency signals\nParticularly useful for imaging soft tissues.\nUnlike X-ray/CT, MRI does not rely on ionising radiation."},
{"no":109,"text":"In 2003, Alison Richard became the first-ever woman Vice-Chancellor of:","A":"Oxford University","B":"Cambridge University","C":"Harvard University","D":"Purdue University","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Cambridge University","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Alison Richard became the first woman to serve as Vice-Chancellor of the University of Cambridge.\nShe took office in 2003.","revisionPoints":"Alison Richard → University of Cambridge.\nCambridge → one of the historic universities of the United Kingdom.\nCambridge and Oxford are collectively known as Oxbridge.\nQuestions may combine personalities with major international institutions."},
{"no":110,"text":"Nine different letters are to be dropped into three different letter boxes. In how many different ways can this be done?","A":"27","B":"39","C":"9³","D":"3⁹","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Each of the 9 different letters can be placed independently into any one of the 3 boxes.\nTherefore, each letter has 3 choices.\nTotal arrangements:\n3 × 3 × 3 × ... (9 times)\n= 3⁹","revisionPoints":"Independent choices → use the multiplication principle.\nIf each of n objects has r possible destinations:\nTotal = rⁿ\nHere:\nObjects = 9\nBoxes = 3\nWays = 3⁹"},
{"no":111,"text":"George W. Bush, the President of America, comes from which of the following American States?","A":"California","B":"Texas","C":"Virginia","D":"Indiana","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"USA","tags":["George W. Bush","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"George W. Bush had strong political and personal associations with Texas.\nHe served as Governor of Texas before becoming President of the United States.","revisionPoints":"Texas → southern United States.\nCapital → Austin.\nLargest city → Houston.\nTexas is historically important for:\nEnergy\nAgriculture\nIndustry\nSpace activities"},
{"no":112,"text":"Which of the following pairs is correctly matched?\nDepartment of Women and Child Development — Ministry of Health and Family\nDepartment of Official Language — Ministry of Human Resource\nDepartment of Drinking Water Supply — Ministry of Water Resources","A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Union Ministries","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source identifies none as correctly matched.\nThe departments mentioned were associated with different ministries from those given in the question.\nTherefore, none of the three pairs is correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Union government departments can be reorganised or shifted between ministries.\nAlways distinguish:\nMinistry\nDepartment\nStatutory body\nConstitutional body\nDepartmental structures are particularly time-sensitive in current-affairs questions."},
{"no":113,"text":"Match the agencies with their headquarters:\nList I – Agency\nList II – Headquarters\nA. UNDP\n1. Nairobi\nB. UNEP\n2. Vienna\nC. UNIDO\n3. Berne\nD. UPU\n4. New York\nCodes:","A":"2–3–4–1","B":"4–1–2–3","C":"2–1–4–3","D":"4–3–2–1","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"United Nations","tags":["UN Agencies","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"UNDP → New York\nUNEP → Nairobi\nUNIDO → Vienna\nUPU → Berne\nTherefore, the correct sequence is 4–1–2–3.","revisionPoints":"UNDP → United Nations Development Programme → New York.\nUNEP → United Nations Environment Programme → Nairobi.\nUNIDO → United Nations Industrial Development Organization → Vienna.\nUPU → Universal Postal Union → Berne, Switzerland.\nHeadquarters-based questions are common in international organisations."},
{"no":114,"text":"Which of the following authors won the Booker Prize twice?","A":"Margaret Atwood","B":"J. M. Coetzee","C":"Graham Swift","D":"Ian McEwan","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Awards","tags":["Booker Prize","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"J. M. Coetzee won the Booker Prize twice:\n1983 → Life & Times of Michael K\n1999 → Disgrace\nHe became the first author to win the prize twice.","revisionPoints":"J. M. Coetzee → South African-born writer.\nBooker-winning novels:\nLife & Times of Michael K\nDisgrace\nBooker Prize → major literary prize for fiction."},
{"no":115,"text":"In the well-known lawn tennis doubles team, Max Mirnyi, partner of Mahesh Bhupathi, comes from which country?","A":"Italy","B":"Sweden","C":"Belarus","D":"Croatia","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Tennis","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Max Mirnyi was a professional tennis player from Belarus.\nHe was known for his doubles career and partnered Mahesh Bhupathi.","revisionPoints":"Max Mirnyi → Belarus.\nMahesh Bhupathi → India.\nBelarus → Eastern Europe.\nCapital → Minsk."},
{"no":116,"text":"Which cricketer holds the record for the highest score in a Test Match innings by an Indian?","A":"Sunil Gavaskar","B":"Vinoo Mankad","C":"Sachin Tendulkar","D":"Virender Sehwag","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Cricket","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Virender Sehwag scored 309 against Pakistan in 2004.\nThis made him the first Indian to score a triple century in Test cricket.\nThe question reflects the record status at the time of the 2004 paper.","revisionPoints":"Virender Sehwag → explosive opening batsman.\nFirst Indian Test triple-centurion → 309.\nTriple century → 300 or more runs in a Test innings.\nCricket records are time-sensitive and can subsequently change."},
{"no":117,"text":"INS Trishul, acquired by the Indian Navy in 2003, was built by:","A":"Israel","B":"USA","C":"Russia","D":"France","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["Indian Navy","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"INS Trishul was a Talwar-class frigate.\nIt was built in Russia and subsequently inducted into the Indian Navy.","revisionPoints":"INS Trishul → Indian Navy frigate.\nTalwar-class frigates were built with Russian assistance.\nIndia–Russia defence cooperation has historically included:\nNaval platforms\nAircraft\nMissiles\nArmoured systems"},
{"no":118,"text":"INSAT-3E, India's communication satellite, was launched in 2003 from:","A":"French Guiana","B":"Seychelles","C":"Mauritius","D":"Mauritania","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["INSAT","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"INSAT-3E was launched in 2003 using an Ariane launch vehicle.\nThe launch took place from the Guiana Space Centre in French Guiana.\nEuropean launch services were used for this satellite.","revisionPoints":"French Guiana → northeastern South America.\nGuiana Space Centre → major international spaceport.\nIndia has used foreign launch services for some heavier communication satellites.\nINSAT → major Indian satellite system for:\nCommunication\nBroadcasting\nMeteorology\nTelecommunications"},
{"no":119,"text":"Match the persons with their positions:\nPerson\nPosition\nA. Anil Kakodkar\n1. Chief of the Integrated Defence Staff\nB. Raman Puri\n2. Chairman, 17th Law Commission\nC. M. Jagannatha Rao\n3. Chairman, ISRO\nD. G. Madhavan Nair\n4. Chairman, Atomic Energy Commission\nCodes:","A":"3–1–2–4","B":"4–2–1–3","C":"3–2–1–4","D":"4–1–2–3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Personalities in News","tags":["Notable Personalities","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Anil Kakodkar → Chairman, Atomic Energy Commission\nRaman Puri → Chairman, 17th Law Commission\nM. Jagannatha Rao → Chief of the Integrated Defence Staff\nG. Madhavan Nair → Chairman, ISRO","revisionPoints":"Anil Kakodkar → nuclear scientist; Atomic Energy Commission.\nG. Madhavan Nair → space scientist; ISRO.\nLaw Commission → examines and recommends legal reforms.\nIntegrated Defence Staff → facilitates coordination among the armed forces."},
{"no":120,"text":"Consider the following statements about Sikh Gurus:\nBanda Bahadur was appointed as the military leader of the Sikhs by Guru Tegh Bahadur.\nGuru Arjan Dev became the Sikh Guru after Guru Ram Das.\nGuru Arjan Dev gave the Sikhs their own script — Gurmukhi.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1 and 2","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Sikh Gurus","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Banda Singh Bahadur was associated with Guru Gobind Singh, not Guru Tegh Bahadur.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Guru Arjan Dev succeeded Guru Ram Das.\nStatement 3 — The source treats this as correct.\nTherefore, the source answer is 2 and 3.","revisionPoints":"Guru Arjan Dev → fifth Sikh Guru.\nGuru Ram Das → fourth Sikh Guru.\nGuru Gobind Singh → tenth Sikh Guru.\nBanda Singh Bahadur → important Sikh military leader associated with Guru Gobind Singh.\nGurmukhi is the script traditionally associated with Punjabi and Sikh literature."},
{"no":121,"text":"Consider the following Viceroys of India during the British rule:\nLord Curzon\nLord Chelmsford\nLord Hardinge\nLord Irwin\nWhich one of the following is the correct chronological order of their tenure?","A":"1, 3, 2, 4","B":"2, 4, 1, 3","C":"1, 4, 2, 3","D":"2, 3, 1, 4","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Viceroys of India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Lord Curzon — Viceroy from 1899 to 1905.\nLord Hardinge — Viceroy from 1910 to 1916.\nLord Chelmsford — Viceroy from 1916 to 1921.\nLord Irwin — Viceroy from 1926 to 1931.\nTherefore: Curzon → Hardinge → Chelmsford → Irwin.","revisionPoints":"Curzon — Partition of Bengal, 1905.\nHardinge — Capital shifted from Calcutta to Delhi in 1911.\nChelmsford — Government of India Act, 1919.\nIrwin — Civil Disobedience Movement and Gandhi-Irwin Pact."},
{"no":122,"text":"Consider the following events during India’s freedom struggle:\nChauri Chaura Outrage\nMinto-Morley Reforms\nDandi March\nMontague-Chelmsford Reforms\nWhich one of the following is the correct chronological order?","A":"1, 3, 2, 4","B":"2, 4, 1, 3","C":"1, 4, 2, 3","D":"2, 3, 1, 4","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Freedom Struggle Timeline","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Minto-Morley Reforms — 1909.\nMontagu-Chelmsford Reforms — 1919.\nChauri Chaura incident — 1922.\nDandi March — 1930.\nHence: 2 → 4 → 1 → 3.","revisionPoints":"1909 — Indian Councils Act / Minto-Morley Reforms.\n1919 — Government of India Act.\n1922 — Chauri Chaura incident led to withdrawal of Non-Cooperation Movement.\n1930 — Dandi March inaugurated Civil Disobedience Movement."},
{"no":123,"text":"Consider the following events:\nFourth general elections in India\nFormation of Haryana State\nMysore named as Karnataka State\nMeghalaya and Tripura become full States\nWhich one of the following is the correct chronological order?","A":"2, 1, 4, 3","B":"4, 3, 2, 1","C":"2, 3, 4, 1","D":"4, 1, 2, 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"States Reorganisation","tags":["Indian States Timeline","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Haryana formed — 1966.\nFourth General Elections — 1967.\nMeghalaya and Tripura became full States — 1972.\nMysore renamed Karnataka — 1973.\nTherefore: 2 → 1 → 4 → 3.","revisionPoints":"Haryana was created from Punjab in 1966.\nFourth Lok Sabha elections were held in 1967.\nMeghalaya became a full state in 1972.\nMysore State was renamed Karnataka in 1973."},
{"no":124,"text":"Match the fuel gases with their major constituents:\nFuel Gas\nMajor Constituents\nA. CNG\n1. Carbon monoxide, Hydrogen\nB. Coal gas\n2. Butane, Propane\nC. LPG\n3. Methane, Ethane\nD. Water gas\n4. Hydrogen, Methane, Carbon monoxide","A":"2, 1, 3, 4","B":"3, 4, 2, 1","C":"2, 4, 3, 1","D":"3, 1, 2, 4","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Fuel Gases","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"CNG → Methane, Ethane\nCoal gas → Hydrogen, Methane, Carbon monoxide\nLPG → Propane, Butane\nWater gas → Carbon monoxide, Hydrogen\nHence: 3, 4, 2, 1.","revisionPoints":"CNG = Compressed Natural Gas.\nLPG mainly contains propane and butane.\nWater gas mainly contains CO + H₂.\nCoal gas is a mixture containing hydrogen, methane and carbon monoxide."},
{"no":125,"text":"A spherical body moves with a uniform angular velocity around a circular path of radius r. Which statement is correct?","A":"The body has no acceleration.","B":"The body has a radial acceleration ω²r directed towards the centre of the path.","C":"The body has a radial acceleration ω²r directed away from the centre.","D":"The body has an acceleration ω²r tangential to its path.","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Circular Motion","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Uniform circular motion involves continuous change in velocity direction.\nTherefore, the body has centripetal acceleration.\nIts magnitude is:a = ω²r\nDirection is always towards the centre of the circular path.","revisionPoints":"Centripetal acceleration: a = v²/r = ω²r.\nDirection: towards centre.\nVelocity: tangential to the circle.\nCentripetal acceleration is perpendicular to instantaneous velocity."},
{"no":126,"text":"‘Deccan Odyssey’ is:","A":"A book on Chhatrapati Shivaji","B":"A warship acquired by the Indian Navy","C":"An air service between Mumbai and Colombo","D":"A luxury train which travels through Maharashtra and includes Goa in its journey","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Tourism","tags":["Deccan Odyssey","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Deccan Odyssey is a luxury tourist train.\nIt was associated with tourism in Maharashtra.\nIts itinerary includes destinations in Maharashtra and Goa.","revisionPoints":"Deccan Odyssey — luxury tourist train.\nAssociated with Maharashtra tourism.\nQuestions on tourism infrastructure can connect transport with regional tourism."},
{"no":127,"text":"Standard 18-carat gold sold in the market contains:","A":"82 parts gold and 18 parts other metals","B":"18 parts gold and 82 parts other metals","C":"18 parts gold and 6 parts other metals","D":"9 parts gold and 15 parts other metals","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"General Science","tags":["Gold Purity","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Pure gold is 24 carat.\n18-carat gold means 18 parts gold out of 24.\nRemaining = 24 − 18 = 6 parts other metals.\nGold purity = 18/24 = 75%.","revisionPoints":"24-carat = pure gold.\n18-carat = 75% gold.\n22-carat = 22/24 gold.\nOther metals are added to improve hardness and durability."},
{"no":128,"text":"A weightless rubber balloon is filled with 200 cc of water. Its weight in water is equal to:","A":"9.8/5 N","B":"9.8/10 N","C":"9.8/2 N","D":"Zero","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Archimedes Principle","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The source treats the balloon as weightless.\nWhen immersed in water, the upward buoyant force acts on it.\nUnder the source's framing, the apparent weight becomes zero.","revisionPoints":"Buoyant force acts upward.\nArchimedes’ principle relates buoyancy to displaced fluid.\nApparent weight = actual weight − buoyant force.\nA weightless object with equal upward support has zero apparent weight."},
{"no":129,"text":"Salts of which elements provide colours to fireworks?","A":"Zinc and sulphur","B":"Potassium and mercury","C":"Strontium and barium","D":"Chromium and nickel","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Fireworks","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Different metal salts produce characteristic colours when heated.\nStrontium salts → red colour.\nBarium salts → green colour.\nHence the correct pair is strontium and barium.","revisionPoints":"Strontium → red.\nBarium → green.\nCopper compounds → blue/blue-green.\nSodium compounds → yellow."},
{"no":130,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nReserve Bank of India was nationalised on 26 January 1950.\nThe borrowing programme of the Government of India is handled by the Department of Expenditure, Ministry of Finance.\nWhich statement(s) is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Reserve Bank of India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: RBI was nationalised on 1 January 1949, not 26 January 1950.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The source identifies the Department of Economic Affairs, rather than Department of Expenditure, in relation to the government's borrowing programme.\nTherefore, neither statement is correct.","revisionPoints":"RBI established in 1935.\nRBI was nationalised in 1949.\nRBI is India's central bank.\nDepartment of Economic Affairs is part of the Ministry of Finance."},
{"no":131,"text":"Which is the correct decreasing sequence in terms of the value of minerals produced in India in 2002–03?","A":"Metallic minerals → Fuel minerals → Non-metallic minerals","B":"Fuel minerals → Metallic minerals → Non-metallic minerals","C":"Metallic minerals → Non-metallic minerals → Fuel minerals","D":"Fuel minerals → Non-metallic minerals → Metallic minerals","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Minerals of India","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"According to the source data for 2002–03:\nFuel minerals had the highest value.\nMetallic minerals came next.\nNon-metallic minerals followed.\nHence the sequence is Fuel → Metallic → Non-metallic.","revisionPoints":"Fuel minerals include coal, petroleum and natural gas.\nMetallic minerals include iron ore, bauxite, manganese etc.\nNon-metallic minerals include limestone, mica, gypsum etc.\nMineral classification can be based on economic and physical characteristics."},
{"no":132,"text":"Which city is not correctly matched with its country?","A":"Salamanca — Spain","B":"Cannes — Italy","C":"Cancun — Mexico","D":"Bruges — Belgium","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Cities in News","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Salamanca → Spain — correct.\nCannes → France, not Italy.\nCancun → Mexico — correct.\nBruges → Belgium — correct.","revisionPoints":"Cannes is in southern France.\nSalamanca is in Spain.\nCancun is a major tourist destination in Mexico.\nBruges is a historic city in Belgium."},
{"no":133,"text":"As per the 2001 Census:\nThe two States with the lowest sex ratio are Haryana and Punjab.\nThe two States with the lowest population per sq. km are Meghalaya and Mizoram.\nKerala has both the highest literacy rate and sex ratio.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"3 only","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 2","D":"1 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Census","tags":["Census 2001","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source's 2001 Census framing.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The source does not support Meghalaya and Mizoram as the two lowest-density States.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Kerala had the highest literacy rate and highest sex ratio among Indian States in the 2001 Census.\nTherefore: 1 and 3.","revisionPoints":"Sex ratio = number of females per 1,000 males.\nKerala has historically had a very high sex ratio.\nCensus data questions require attention to the census year.\nPopulation density = population per square kilometre."},
{"no":134,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIn the Third Battle of Panipat, Ahmad Shah Abdali defeated Ibrahim Lodi.\nTipu Sultan was killed in the Third Anglo-Mysore War.\nMir Jafar conspired with the English for the defeat of Nawab Sirajuddaulah in the Battle of Plassey.\nWhich statement(s) is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Battle of Plassey","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Ahmad Shah Abdali defeated the Marathas, not Ibrahim Lodi, at the Third Battle of Panipat.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: Tipu Sultan was killed in the Fourth Anglo-Mysore War in 1799.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Mir Jafar was involved in the conspiracy against Sirajuddaulah before the Battle of Plassey.\nHence only 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Third Battle of Panipat — 1761.\nAbdali vs Marathas.\nBattle of Plassey — 1757.\nSirajuddaulah was defeated by the English East India Company.\nTipu Sultan died in 1799."},
{"no":135,"text":"Among the following Indian States, which has the minimum total forest cover?","A":"Sikkim","B":"Goa","C":"Haryana","D":"Kerala","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Forestry","tags":["Forest Cover","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The question asks for total forest cover, not the percentage of geographical area under forest.\nAccording to the source's data, Haryana has the minimum total forest cover among the given options.","revisionPoints":"Distinguish forest cover from forest-cover percentage.\nSmall States may have a high percentage of forest cover but a small absolute area.\nHaryana is predominantly an agricultural and highly cultivated State."},
{"no":136,"text":"How many three-digit even numbers are there such that 9 comes as a succeeding digit only when 7 is the preceding digit and 7 is the preceding digit only when 9 is the succeeding digit?","A":"120","B":"210","C":"365","D":"405","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The condition links the occurrence of 7 and 9.\nIf 9 occurs, it must be immediately associated with a preceding 7.\nIf 7 occurs, it must be followed by 9.\nThus the relevant pair is 79.\nThe number must also be three-digit and even.\nThe supplied source gives 405 as the answer.","revisionPoints":"Questions involving digit restrictions should be solved by:\nidentifying mandatory digit patterns,\napplying positional restrictions,\nthen applying the even-number condition.\nFor an even number, the units digit must be 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8.\nTreat linked digit conditions carefully before counting."},
{"no":137,"text":"Match the sportsperson with the sport:\nSportsperson\nSport\nA. Shikha Tandon\n1. Badminton\nB. Ignace Tirkey\n2. Swimming\nC. Pankaj Advani\n3. Lawn Tennis\nD. Rohan Bopanna\n4. Snooker\n5. Hockey","A":"3, 5, 4, 2","B":"2, 4, 1, 3","C":"3, 4, 1, 2","D":"2, 5, 4, 3","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Sports Personalities","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Shikha Tandon → Swimming\nIgnace Tirkey → Hockey\nPankaj Advani → Snooker\nRohan Bopanna → Lawn Tennis\nTherefore: 2, 5, 4, 3.","revisionPoints":"Shikha Tandon — swimming.\nIgnace Tirkey — hockey.\nPankaj Advani — cue sports.\nRohan Bopanna — lawn tennis.\nMatch-the-following questions often combine personalities with sports, awards or institutions."},
{"no":138,"text":"The record for the highest score in an innings in Test Cricket is held by Brian Lara. Immediately prior to him, the three record holders were:","A":"Don Bradman, Sunil Gavaskar and Colin Cowdrey","B":"Len Hutton, Peter May and Vivian Richards","C":"Hayden, Lara, Sobers","D":"Bob Cowper, Bill Lawry and Brian Lara","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Cricket","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Gary Sobers — 365*.\nBrian Lara — 375.\nMatthew Hayden — 380.\nBrian Lara subsequently regained the record with 400*.\nThus the sequence immediately preceding Lara's 400* record was Hayden → Lara → Sobers.","revisionPoints":"Gary Sobers — 365*.\nBrian Lara — 375.\nMatthew Hayden — 380.\nBrian Lara — 400*.\nSports records are time-sensitive and should always be understood with the relevant year."},
{"no":139,"text":"In how many different ways can six players be arranged in a line such that two of them, Ajit and Mukherjee, are never together?","A":"120","B":"240","C":"360","D":"480","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Total arrangements of 6 players:\n6! = 720\nTreat Ajit and Mukherjee as one block:\n5 objects can be arranged in 5! ways.\nAjit and Mukherjee can interchange positions in 2! ways.\nTogether = 5! × 2 = 240\nNever together:\n720 − 240 = 480","revisionPoints":"Total permutations of n distinct objects = n!.\nFor two specified persons together:\nTreat them as one block.\nInternal arrangements of the block = 2!.\n“Never together” = Total − Together."},
{"no":140,"text":"Match the State/Province/Overseas Territory with its country:\nRegion\nCountry\nA. British Columbia\n1. USA\nB. Bavaria\n2. UK\nC. Gibraltar\n3. Canada\nD. Rhode Island\n4. Germany\n5. Denmark","A":"1, 2, 5, 3","B":"3, 4, 2, 1","C":"1, 4, 2, 3","D":"3, 2, 5, 1","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Provinces & Territories","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"British Columbia → Canada\nBavaria → Germany\nGibraltar → UK\nRhode Island → USA\nTherefore: 3, 4, 2, 1.","revisionPoints":"British Columbia — Canada.\nBavaria — Germany.\nGibraltar — British Overseas Territory.\nRhode Island — United States.\nMatch-the-following geography questions often test political geography rather than physical geography."},
{"no":141,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nFemur is the longest bone in the human body.\nCholera is a disease caused by bacteria.\n‘Athlete’s foot’ is a disease caused by virus.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Human Body","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1: The source marks this question as (b), but the statement itself is factually inconsistent with that key.\nStatement 2: Cholera is caused by the bacterium Vibrio cholerae.\nStatement 3: The source treats this as correct.\nSource-key note: The supplied source answer is (b), although standard biology identifies athlete’s foot as a fungal infection, not a viral disease. Therefore, the source key appears to contain an error.","revisionPoints":"Femur is the longest and strongest bone in the human body.\nCholera → caused by Vibrio cholerae.\nAthlete’s foot → generally caused by fungi, especially dermatophytes.\nDo not confuse bacterial, viral and fungal diseases."},
{"no":142,"text":"50 men or 80 women can finish a job in 50 days. A contractor deploys 40 men and 48 women, but after every 10 days, 5 men and 8 women are removed until the work is completed. The work is completed in:","A":"45 days","B":"50 days","C":"54 days","D":"62 days","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"From the source:\n50 men = 80 women.\nTherefore, 1 woman = 5/8 man in work capacity.\nThe source gives 45 days as the answer.\nSource-key note: Direct work-rate calculation from the stated figures produces a different result, so this question appears to contain an inconsistency in the supplied answer key/data.","revisionPoints":"Convert different worker categories into a common work-rate unit.\nIf:\n50 men = 80 women,\nthen 1 man = 80/50 = 8/5 women.\nWork problems require tracking workforce changes after each specified interval.\nAlways verify the total work completed interval-by-interval."},
{"no":143,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?","A":"Railway Staff College — Vadodara","B":"Central Organization for Railway Electrification — Varanasi","C":"Wheel and Axle Plant — Bangalore","D":"Rail Coach Factory — Kapurthala","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Transport & Infrastructure","tags":["Indian Railways","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Railway Staff College → Vadodara.\nCentral Organization for Railway Electrification → Varanasi.\nWheel and Axle Plant → Bengaluru/Bangalore.\nRail Coach Factory → Kapurthala.\nSource answer: (c).","revisionPoints":"Indian Railways has specialised production and training units at different locations.\nVadodara → Railway Staff College.\nKapurthala → Rail Coach Factory.\nLocation-based railway questions often test institutional geography."},
{"no":144,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nSmart Card is a plastic card with an embedded microchip.\nDigital technology is primarily used with new physical communication media such as satellite and fibre-optic transmission.\nA digital library is a collection of documents in an organised electronic form available on the Internet only.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"3 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Smart Card","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: A smart card contains an embedded electronic chip.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Digital communication is used through technologies such as satellites and fibre-optic systems.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: A digital library is not necessarily available only on the Internet.\nHence, 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Smart card → embedded integrated circuit/chip.\nDigital technology represents information using discrete values.\nFibre optics transmit information using light.\nDigital libraries can contain electronically stored and organised information."},
{"no":145,"text":"Match the new names of countries with their old names:\nNew Name\nOld Name\nA. Benin\n1. Nyasaland\nB. Belize\n2. Basutoland\nC. Botswana\n3. Bechuanaland\nD. Malawi\n4. British Honduras\n5. Dahomey","A":"3, 1, 2, 4","B":"5, 4, 3, 1","C":"3, 4, 2, 1","D":"5, 1, 3, 4","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Country Name Changes","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Benin → Dahomey\nBelize → British Honduras\nBotswana → Bechuanaland\nMalawi → Nyasaland\nTherefore: 5, 4, 3, 1.","revisionPoints":"Dahomey → Benin.\nBritish Honduras → Belize.\nBechuanaland → Botswana.\nNyasaland → Malawi.\nSuch questions test historical geography and colonial-era names."},
{"no":146,"text":"Which is the correct sequence in descending order of precedence in the Warrant of Precedence?","A":"Attorney General → Judges of Supreme Court → Members of Parliament → Deputy Chairman of Rajya Sabha","B":"Judges of Supreme Court → Deputy Chairman of Rajya Sabha → Attorney General → Members of Parliament","C":"Attorney General → Deputy Chairman of Rajya Sabha → Judges of Supreme Court → Members of Parliament","D":"Judges of Supreme Court → Attorney General → Deputy Chairman of Rajya Sabha → Members of Parliament","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Warrant of Precedence","tags":["Warrant of Precedence","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The question concerns the Warrant of Precedence.\nIt establishes relative ceremonial precedence among constitutional authorities.\nAccording to the source, the descending sequence is:\nSupreme Court Judges\nDeputy Chairman of Rajya Sabha\nAttorney General of India\nMembers of Parliament","revisionPoints":"Warrant of Precedence relates to state and ceremonial occasions.\nIt is different from constitutional powers and functions.\nPrecedence does not automatically indicate superior constitutional authority.\nPresident occupies the highest position in India's Warrant of Precedence."},
{"no":147,"text":"Three students are picked at random from a school having a total of 1000 students. The probability that these three students will have identical date and month of birth is:","A":"3/1000","B":"3/365","C":"1/(365)²","D":"1/(365)²","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"The question considers the probability that three randomly selected students share the same date and month of birth.\nThere are approximately 365 possible dates.\nThe supplied source gives 3/365.\nThe options in the source appear to contain a duplication/formatting issue.","revisionPoints":"Probability = favourable outcomes / total outcomes.\nBirth-date problems generally assume:\n365 days,\nequal probability for each date,\nleap years ignored.\nAlways examine whether the question asks for:\nsame birthday,\nat least two sharing a birthday,\nor all three sharing the same birthday."},
{"no":148,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nMontenegro and Serbia agreed to a new structure for the Yugoslav Federation.\nCroatia remained under the Hungarian Administration until the end of First World War.\nClaims to Macedonia Territory have long been a source of contention between Belgium and Greece.\nIn 1991, Slovenia declared independence from Czechoslovakia.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"2, 3 and 4","D":"1, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Balkans","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Serbia and Montenegro agreed to a new constitutional arrangement for their federation.\nStatement 2 — Correct according to the source.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: The relevant dispute concerning Macedonia involved Greece and neighbouring Balkan states, not Belgium.\nStatement 4 — Incorrect: Slovenia declared independence from Yugoslavia, not Czechoslovakia.\nHence: 1 and 2.","revisionPoints":"Slovenia and Croatia declared independence from Yugoslavia in 1991.\nYugoslavia underwent major political fragmentation during the 1990s.\nMacedonia-related disputes are associated with the Balkans.\nDistinguish Yugoslavia from Czechoslovakia in post-Cold War European history."},
{"no":149,"text":"Shirin Ebadi, who won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2003, is from:","A":"Iraq","B":"Nigeria","C":"Iran","D":"Libya","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Nobel Peace Prize","UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Shirin Ebadi is an Iranian lawyer and human-rights advocate.\nShe received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2003.\nShe was recognised for her work concerning democracy and human rights.","revisionPoints":"Shirin Ebadi → Iran.\nNobel Peace Prize → 2003.\nNobel-related questions can test:\nperson,\ncountry,\nyear,\nfield of contribution.\nDistinguish Nobel Peace Prize from other Nobel categories."},
{"no":150,"text":"A person stands at the middle point of a wooden ladder which starts slipping between a vertical wall and the floor of a room, while continuing to remain in a vertical plane. The path traced by a person standing at the middle point of the slipping ladder is:","A":"A straight line","B":"An elliptical path","C":"A circular path","D":"A parabolic path","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2004"],"expl":"Let the ladder length be L.\nIts upper end touches the vertical wall and lower end touches the floor.\nIf the person stands at the middle point, the distance from the corner to the person remains L/2.\nTherefore, as the ladder slips, the midpoint maintains a constant distance from the corner.\nA point maintaining a constant distance from a fixed point traces a circle.","revisionPoints":"The midpoint of a slipping ladder traces a quarter-circle in the relevant region.\nLadder length remains constant.\nThis is a geometry + mechanics application.\nKey idea: constant distance from a fixed point → circular path."},
];
